{"type":"event","event":{"type":"tool_completed","toolCallId":"call_8e081b48eb954c4e91306b58","toolName":"read_file","result":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 57 lines\n1: Created-GMT: 2026-10-01 10:08:08 GMT\n2: Created-Local: 2026-10-01 17:08:08 +07 (+0700)\n3: Coding-Agent: claude\n4: Session-ID: 976059c2-629b-4b91-8b63-7c4d9604d7cf (resumed; the spike engineer)\n5: # Task: Python phase C, slice C1 — finally/with, tuples/slices, operators, comprehensions, keyword arguments, gate P3s\n6: \n7: Role: Yang Compiler and Universal AST Engineer\n8: \n9: Implementers:\n10: - Model: claude-opus-5-5 | Assigned: 2026-10-01 17:08:08 +07 (+0700) | Status: active | Rationale: author of the spike (phases A, B); continuity\n11: \n12: WORK TREE: work ONLY in /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-py-c1 (branch yang-python-phase-c1 from master 60b60898, which\n13: now contains the spike bf6c5544 and heap reclamation 60b60898). Do not touch other worktrees. Do not stage or commit.\n14: \n15: OWNER (verbatim): \"go ahead with 4 and 5\" (plan item 4 = Python phase C). Owner direction (verbatim, binding):\n16: \"integrating antlr should be straight forward ... mapping from antlr AST to it should be a straight forward but tedious\n17: mapping\"; \"if yin.vm universal AST has continuations, all control flow can be mapped to continuations\"; the dynamic\n18: stream pipeline (\"any number of interpreters can attach to those dao.stream to do more transformation of its own\").\n19: Context (this worktree's collab/): your spike brief and final report; the glm r2 gate findings (5 P3s); the Python\n20: mappability ruling (bucket 1 items and owner decisions); the mutable-objects ruling.\n21: \n22: C1 scope (lowering + prelude only; no VM changes; no new AST tag):\n23: 1. finally and with: escape wrappers (dynamic-wind style) so EVERY exit — normal, return, break, continue, raise —\n24:    runs pending finally blocks in order and with's __exit__ (with exception info when raised; a truthy __exit__ result\n25:    suppresses). Handler-stack consistency across nested finally/try/loops; tests for each exit kind and nesting.\n26: 2. Tuples (immutable, value semantics, hashable as dict keys when elements are) and tuple targets / unpacking\n27:    (a, b = ...; for k, v in ...; nested; star target if simple, else rejected); slices on list/tuple/str\n28:    (start/stop/step, negatives, clamping per Python) and slice assignment on lists if simple (else rejected).\n29: 3. Operators: ** (int and float), %, // (Python floor semantics incl. negatives; ZeroDivisionError), bitwise & | ^ ~\n30:    << >> on ints, in / not in (list, tuple, str substring, dict keys, set if present), augmented forms.\n31: 4. Comprehensions: list/dict/set comprehensions and generator expressions consumed eagerly where a generator is not\n32:    observable (e.g. as the sole argument to list/len/sum) — otherwise reject generator expressions until C2;\n33:    Python 3 comprehension scope (own scope; the first iterable evaluated in the enclosing scope).\n34: 5. Keyword arguments at call sites, **kwargs, keyword-only parameters, default/keyword binding errors as TypeError.\n35: 6. The 5 P3 notes from the glm r2 gate: implicit object base and prelude exception classes via the module dict where the\n36:    ruling says so; `global globals; globals()`; out-of-range \\U escapes as a qualified diagnostic; reject leading-zero\n37:    decimal literals (0755) per Python 3; repr deviations (UnboundLocalError.args, control characters) where cheap.\n38: Everything else stays rejected with a qualified diagnostic (generators/yield -> C2; big ints, imports, linked prelude,\n39: REPL catalog -> later slices). Keep the \"every grammar rule classified\" test green and update it.\n40: \n41: Tests: e2e programs for every item on all four VMs over the real cell and data modules; lowering goldens where the shape\n42: matters; each new semantics test must fail when its part is reverted (prove by temporary mutation, then restore).\n43: Portability: every reader conditional in .cljc needs a :cljd branch listed FIRST; protocol methods never use duplicate\n44: parameter names (e.g. [_ _x], not [_ _]) — ClojureDart casts the this-parameter.\n45: \n46: Verify — EVERY command in the FOREGROUND: kondo (files as separate args); cljstyle check per file (say if blocked);\n47: bb build:yin-repl-node then bb gen:python-antlr (needed by the JVM suite in a fresh worktree); focused JVM; full\n48: clj -M:test; bb test:cljs. NOT bb test:cljd.\n49: \n50: Write the report to /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-py-c1/collab/1790849288904-compiler-engineer-python-phase-c1.claude-opus-5-5.report.md\n51: and give it as your final response, beginning exactly with:\n52: Completed-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\n53: Completed-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\n54: Coding-Agent: claude\n55: Session-ID: 976059c2-629b-4b91-8b63-7c4d9604d7cf\n56: Report: what is supported now vs still rejected; the rule->UAST changes; prelude additions; exact test outcomes; the\n57: mutation proof; which constructs were mechanical and which were not; unresolved concerns."}],"deferred":false}}
