apes, leading-zero decimals accepted, cosmetic repr deviations) — none blocking. The one caveat: my CLJD sign-off is by reading; the orchestrator's CLJD lane relay is the executing confirmation still pending.\n7: \n8: ---- full report (from /Users/sto/.claude-glm/plans/read-collab-1790834614568-reviewer-pytho-quirky-cloud.md) ----\n9: # Gate r2 — Python ANTLR spike: read-only review (task complete; no implementation)\n10: \n11: The task (collab/1790834614568-reviewer-python-antlr-spike-gate-r2.prompt.md) is a read-only\n12: adversarial review of the uncommitted spike. It was completed entirely with read-only actions —\n13: there is nothing to implement. The full report is the deliverable and is given in the final\n14: response; verdict **READY**, sign-off **GRANTED** (P3 notes only).\n15: \n16: ## What was verified (evidence in the final response)\n17: \n18: - **P1 fixed + tested**: `lower.cljc:358-368` (`read-global` → `py/global-or`), `prelude.cljc:175-180`\n19:   (`py/global-or`, present module key wins); e2e `builtin-fallback-before-module-binding-test`\n20:   (e2e_test.clj:177) is r1's exact repro on all four VMs; golden `lower_test.clj:65-68` (`builtin`).\n21: - **P2 fixed + tested**: `lower.cljc:376-379` (`py/class-ns-get` + thunk fallback), `prelude.cljc:181-186`;\n22:   e2e `class-body-reads-class-namespace-then-globals-test` (e2e_test.clj:189) is r1's exact repro.\n23: - **8 `:cljd` branches**: exactly 8 reader conditionals in the spike, `:cljd` FIRST in all\n24:   (grep-verified); every idiom has repo precedent (`double/parse` yang/php.cljc:95;\n25:   `StringBuffer`/`writeCharCode` yin/vm/data.cljc:160; `.floor` voxel_scene.cljc:107;\n26:   `.toInt` data.cljc:85; `catch Object` yin/repl/index.cljc:278).\n27: - **Fresh r1 re-review**: unknown rules fail loudly; determinism (no gensym, node-id names);\n28:   no ANTLR imports outside `cst.clj` + dev gen task; no host-map iteration in the prelude;\n29:   no continuation-representation test (flag cells only, grep clean); prelude host names =\n30:   exactly cell/new,get,set! + data/count,into,subvec,str-concat,str-length (all real exports,\n31:   arities match); cells reached via the D4 effect path (module.cljc:329-352); stage outcome\n32:   handling total (unknown outcomes throw); build/manifest: vendored .g4 digests re-verified\n33:   locally (shasum matches manifest), MIT headers verbatim, helper licence recorded UNRESOLVED,\n34:   redistribution block recorded.\n35: \n36: ## Findings (all P3, none blocking)\n37: \n38: 1. P3 — implicit `object` base and prelude-internal exception classes bypass the module dict\n39:    (`lower.cljc:828`, prelude `py/type-error`): shadowing `object`/`TypeError` at module level\n40:    changes Python's behaviour but not the spike's. Subset boundary; source-level reads are correct.\n41: 2. P3 — `def f(): global globals; globals()` is rejected as \"globals used as a value\"\n42:    (`lower.cljc:367` reachable via `builtin?` false) — Python allows it. Ultra-edge.\n43: 3. P3 — `'\\U00110000'`-style escapes raise an unqualified host error (IllegalArgumentException\n44:    from Character/toChars) that escapes the diagnostics port (`lower.cljc:270-274`, 1119-1129).\n45:    Loud, not silent.\n46: 4. P3 — leading-zero decimal literals (`0755`) are accepted as 755; Python 3 rejects them\n47:    (`lower.cljc:254-267`).\n48: 5. P3 — cosmetic repr deviations: `UnboundLocalError.args` is the bare name; control characters\n49:    in nested string repr are unescaped; JVM float repr outside [1e-4, 1e16) (all documented).\n50: \n51: ## Verification\n52: \n53: Review by reading only, per the prompt (\"do not rerun\" orchestrator-verified checks; lanes run in\n54: the orchestrator's seat). Digests re-verified with `shasum -a 256` (read-only). CLJD sign-off on\n55: the 8 branches is by reading; the orchestrator's CLJD lane result is still pending relay."}],"deferred":false}}
{"type":"event","event":{"type":"tool_completed","toolCallId":"call_883a4e72e75949c891478030","toolName":"read_file","result":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 222 lines\n1: Completed-GMT: 2026-10-01 10:55:07 GMT\n2: Completed-Local: 2026-10-01 17:55:07 +07\n3: Coding-Agent: claude\n4: Session-ID: 976059c2-629b-4b91-8b63-7c4d9604d7cf\n5: \n6: # Python phase C, slice C1: report\n7: \n8: ## Summary\n9: \n10: All six C1 items are implemented in the lowering and prelude only. There are no VM changes and no new AST tags.\n11: \n12: **Test results.**\n13: - All Python tests pass: 98 tests and 395 assertions, including 13 new end-to-end tests that run on all four VMs over the real `cell` and `data` modules.\n14: - The full JVM suite passes: 2695 tests, 187529 assertions, 0 failures.\n15: - `bb test:cljs` passes with 0 warnings.\n16: \n17: **Mutation proof.** I made 9 deliberate breakages, each reverting one implemented piece. Every one turned its tests red. One of them first exposed a coverage gap in the slice tests, which I closed.\n18: \n19: **Not done.**\n20: - cljstyle is blocked by permission in this session.\n21: - `bb test:cljd` was not run, per the brief.\n22: \n23: Work is in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-py-c1`, on branch `yang-python-phase-c1`. Nothing is staged or committed.\n24: \n25: ## Supported now\n26: \n27: **1. `finally` and `with`.**\n28: - `try`/`finally` and `try`/`except`/`else`/`finally` run the finally block on every exit: normal completion, `return`, `break`, `continue` and `raise`.\n29: - Nested finally blocks run innermost first.\n30: - A `return` inside `finally` overrides the pending return or exception.\n31: - An exception raised inside `finally` replaces the one already in flight.\n32: - `with a as x, b:` follows the language reference's expansion:\n33:   - `__exit__` is looked up before `__enter__` runs;\n34:   - on an exception, `__exit__(type, value, None)` is called, and a truthy result suppresses the exception;\n35:   - every other exit calls `__exit__(None, None, None)`;\n36:   - the `as` target can be any assignment target.\n37: \n38: **2. Tuples, unpacking and slices.**\n39: - Tuples have value semantics:\n40:   - `==` compares elements (so `(1, 2) == (1.0, 2)`);\n41:   - they are hashable when their elements are, so `d[1, 2]` and `d[(1.0, 2)]` find the same key;\n42:   - lists, dicts and sets used as keys raise TypeError \"unhashable type\".\n43: - Tuple and list targets unpack, nested to any depth, including `x, (y, z) = ...`, `[d] = ...`, `for k, v in d.items():` and `with m as (p, q):`.\n44: - One starred target per assignment is allowed (`first, *rest = ...`).\n45: - Unpacking with the wrong count raises ValueError.\n46: - Slices work on lists, tuples and strings, with start, stop and step, negative indexes, and clamping exactly as `slice.indices` does it.\n47: - Slice assignment works on lists for an absent step or a step of 1. An extended slice target is rejected.\n48: \n49: **3. Operators.**\n50: - `**` on ints, and on floats with an integral exponent. A negative exponent returns a float, and `0 ** -n` raises ZeroDivisionError.\n51: - `%` and `//` use Python's floor semantics for negative operands, on both ints and floats, and raise ZeroDivisionError on a zero divisor.\n52: - `& | ^ ~ << >>` on ints, using unbounded two's complement as Python does. A negative shift count raises ValueError.\n53: - `in` and `not in` work for lists, tuples, ranges, str (substring), dict keys and sets.\n54: - All the new augmented forms work. `+=` and `*=` on a list mutate it in place, so aliases see the change, matching `list.__iadd__`.\n55: - Also added: list, tuple and str concatenation and repetition.\n56: \n57: **4. Comprehensions.**\n58: - List, dict and set comprehensions, with multiple `for` and `if` clauses.\n59: - Each comprehension has its own scope:\n60:   - its targets are cells local to the comprehension;\n61:   - the first iterable is evaluated in the enclosing scope, so a class body can use its own names there;\n62:   - lambdas inside the comprehension share the loop variable's single cell, so `[lambda: i for i in range(3)]` gives 2, 2, 2.\n63: - A generator expression is accepted only as the sole argument of `list`, `tuple`, `set`, `sum`, `any` or `all`, and only when that name resolves to the builtin. There it is lowered as a list, which is not observably different. Any other generator expression is rejected as \"generator expression (phase C2)\".\n64: \n65: **5. Keyword arguments.**\n66: - Call sites accept `name=value`, `*iterable` and `**mapping`.\n67: - Definitions accept keyword-only parameters (after `*name` or a bare `*`), keyword-only defaults, and `**kwargs`.\n68: - All binding failures are Python TypeErrors: too many positional arguments, multiple values for an argument, an unexpected keyword, a missing positional argument, a missing keyword-only argument, and `**` applied to a non-mapping or with non-string keys.\n69: - The static syntax errors are: a repeated keyword, a positional argument after a keyword argument, named arguments missing after a bare `*`, and a non-default argument after a default one.\n70: \n71: **6. The five P3 notes from the r2 gate.**\n72: - **(a) Implicit `object` base and the exception classes the prelude raises: left unchanged, because the current behaviour is CPython's.** CPython uses `builtins.object` for an implicit base, and its interpreter-raised errors are `builtins.TypeError` and friends regardless of what the module binds those names to. A test now pins it: after `TypeError = None; object = 1`, a class still defines and an internal TypeError is still caught by `except Exception`.\n73: - **(b) `global globals; globals()` now works.** `globals` is a per-module function object, passed to the module body as `%globals-fn` and read through the same \"module key wins, else builtin\" path as every other builtin. It can also be used as a value now.\n74: - **(c) An out-of-range `\\U` escape is now a qualified syntax diagnostic** (\"illegal Unicode character in \\U escape\"), not a host exception.\n75: - **(d) `0755`: the r2 note does not hold for the pinned grammar.** Its lexer has no token for that literal, so the parser already emits a syntax-error packet; a test now pins that. I added a matching lowering guard in case a grammar ever passes one through.\n76: - **(e) repr fixes:**\n77:   - UnboundLocalError's message is now CPython 3.11's: \"cannot access local variable 'x' where it is not associated with a value\".\n78:   - repr picks quotes the way Python does: `\"it's\"` gets double quotes.\n79:   - `\\n`, `\\r` and `\\t` are escaped, and other control characters as `\\xNN`.\n80: \n81: ## Still rejected (each with a qualified diagnostic)\n82: \n83: - **For C2:** generator expressions anywhere other than the consuming-builtin position, and `yield`.\n84: - **Imports and statements:** imports, `del`, `assert`, `match`, `async`/`await`, and decorators.\n85: - **Display and argument forms:** `@` and `@=`, `{**d}` in a display, multi-dimensional slicing, extended slice assignment, and multiple inheritance.\n86: - **Literals:** f-strings, bytes, and integers above 2^53 (big ints are a later slice).\n87: - **Annotations:** parameter and return annotations.\n88: - **`**` with a non-integral exponent** (for example `2 ** 0.5`). This is a runtime NotImplementedError, not a static rejection, because the runtime exponent is not known statically and there is no portable `pow`/`exp`.\n89: \n90: ## Rule → Universal AST changes\n91: \n92: The \"every grammar rule classified\" test passes and has been updated. These rules moved out of the unsupported set:\n93: \n94: | Rule | Now | Lowers to |\n95: |---|---|---|\n96: | `with_stmt` | handled | `(py/with mgr (fn [%wv] (assign target %wv) body))`, nested right to left for several items |\n97: | `with_item` | consumed by `with_stmt` | (see `with_stmt`) |\n98: | `star_expr` | handled | splice `py/extend` in displays and in `*` call arguments; `py/unpack-star` in targets; a syntax error anywhere else |\n99: | `sliceop` | consumed by `subscript_` | `(py/slice start stop step)` |\n100: | `comp_for`, `comp_iter`, `comp_if` | consumed by the comprehension | `py/for-each` loops and `if` filters inside the comprehension |\n101: | `test_nocond` | handled | lowered to its single child |\n102: | `lambdef_nocond` | handled | same as `lambdef` |\n103: \n104: Existing rules changed as follows:\n105: \n106: | Rule | Change |\n107: |---|---|\n108: | `try_stmt` | A `finally` clause becomes `(py/try-finally (fn [] <try/except part>) (fn [%fx] <finally>))`. |\n109: | `testlist`, `testlist_star_expr`, `exprlist` | One element is lowered as before. Several elements, a trailing comma or a star produce `(py/tuple <elements>)`. A lone `*a` is a syntax error. |\n110: | `atom` | `()` is an empty tuple; a parenthesised list with commas is a tuple; `[...]` and `{...}` cover list, set and dict displays and comprehensions. |\n111: | `expr` | Adds `// % ** & \\| ^ << >>` and unary `~`. A `**` chain is regrouped to the right, because the grammar's left-recursive alternative groups `a**b**c` as `(a**b)**c`. |\n112: | `comparison` | Adds `in` → `py/in` and `not in` → `py/not-in`. |\n113: | `atom_expr` | The call trailer builds positional args with conj and extend, and kwargs pairs with conj and kw-extend. A call with keywords uses `py/call-kw`. A generator-expression argument becomes a list comprehension when the callee is a consuming builtin. The subscript trailer handles slices and tuple keys. |\n114: | assignment, `for` targets, `with` targets | General `assign-target`: tuple and list targets go through `py/unpack` / `py/unpack-star` and are assigned left to right. A tuple target in augmented assignment is a syntax error. |\n115: | `funcdef`, `lambdef` | `py/make-function name spec defaults kwdefaults code`. `spec` is the static `{:params :star? :kwonly :kwstar?}`. Keyword-only defaults are evaluated at definition time. The code vector follows the written order of the parameters. |\n116: | module | `(py/run-module (fn [%globals %globals-fn] body))` |\n117: \n118: ## Prelude additions\n119: \n120: - **Handler stack and escapes.** The stack is now a chain of depth-numbered frames `[kind payload rest depth]`.\n121:   - `py/frame` and `py/frame-depth` build and read them.\n122:   - `py/raise` pops `:finally` frames, running each thunk with the exception, until it reaches a `:handler`.\n123:   - `py/unwind-to` pops frames above a depth, running finally thunks with None. Every `py/call-ec` escape calls it before jumping, so `return`, `break` and `continue` run pending finally blocks.\n124:   - Also new: `py/try-finally`, `py/with`, `py/type-of`.\n125: - **Calls.**\n126:   - `py/make-function` now takes `(name spec defaults kwdefaults code)`.\n127:   - `py/call-kw` is new, and `py/call` is now its positional-only form.\n128:   - Binding is done by `py/bind-args` together with `py/bind-keyword(s)`, `py/fill-defaults`, `py/fill-kwdefaults`, `py/pad`, `py/index-of`, `py/lookup-pair` and `py/fn-error`.\n129:   - Call-site splicing uses `py/extend`, `py/kw-extend` and `py/kw-pairs`.\n130:   - `py/instantiate` passes keyword arguments on to `__init__`.\n131: - **Integer operations.** These are exact algorithms built only from `+ - * <`, because no host primitive does them portably. They are valid within 2^53.\n132:   - `py/divmod-pos` divides by doubling the divisor.\n133:   - `py/int-floordiv` and `py/int-mod` apply Python's sign rule on top of it.\n134:   - `py/floor` floors a real by binary descent; it raises OverflowError outside ±2^53.\n135:   - `py/ipow` raises to an integer power by repeated squaring.\n136:   - `py/bit-op` handles unbounded two's complement by recursing until both operands are 0 or -1.\n137:   - Built on these: `py/floordiv`, `py/mod`, `py/pow`, `py/bitand`, `py/bitor`, `py/bitxor`, `py/invert`, `py/lshift`, `py/rshift`.\n138: - **Sequences.**\n139:   - `py/add` gains list and tuple concatenation.\n140:   - `py/mul` and `py/repeat` handle repetition.\n141:   - `py/iadd` and `py/imul` work in place on lists.\n142:   - `py/kind` and `py/sequence?` classify values.\n143: - **Membership and equality.** `py/contains`, `py/in`, `py/not-in` and `py/seq-contains?` are new. `py/eq` now compares tuples element by element and sets by membership.\n144: - **Keys, sets and dicts.**\n145:   - `py/key` now normalizes tuples recursively and refuses unhashable values; `py/keys-of` supports it.\n146:   - `py/dict-has?`.\n147:   - Sets: `py/set-new`, `py/set-add`, `py/set-from`, `py/set-fill`.\n148:   - Dict methods `items`, `keys`, `values` and `get`. `items`, `keys` and `values` return lists, which are snapshots rather than live views.\n149: - **Slices.** `py/slice`, `py/slice-positions` (the `slice.indices` algorithm), `py/slice-bound`, `py/slice-walk`, `py/pick`, `py/slice-of` and `py/set-slice`. `py/getitem` and `py/setitem` now handle slices and tuples.\n150: - **Iteration.**\n151:   - `py/iterable` turns a str into a tuple of its characters, decoding it once.\n152:   - `py/to-vector`, `py/collect`, `py/for-each` and `py/for-each-at`.\n153:   - `py/unpack` and `py/unpack-star`.\n154:   - `py/iter-at` now also walks sets.\n155: - **Builtins.**\n156:   - New: `list`, `tuple`, `set`, `sum`, `any`, `all`, list `append` and set `add`.\n157:   - Every builtin now carries a parameter spec, so arity and keyword errors are TypeErrors.\n158:   - New exception classes: NotImplementedError and OverflowError.\n159:   - Classes now have a `__name__` attribute.\n160: - **Rendering.** The boundary renderer handles sets (with `set()` for an empty set) and implements the Python string repr.\n161: \n162: ## Test outcomes (all run in the foreground)\n163: \n164: | Check | Result |\n165: |---|---|\n166: | clj-kondo on the 10 changed files, as separate arguments | 0 errors, 0 warnings |\n167: | cljstyle | **not run.** `cljstyle check src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc` returned \"This command requires approval\", so formatting is unverified. |\n168: | `bb build:yin-repl-node`, then `bb gen:python-antlr` | Build completed with 0 warnings. The parser generated with all digests verified. |\n169: | Focused JVM: the seven `yang.python.antlr.*` namespaces | 98 tests, 395 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors |\n170: | Full `clj -M:test` | 2695 tests, 187529 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors |\n171: | `bb test:cljs` | 0 warnings; 2520 tests, 53195 assertions, 0 failures; `lower-portable-test` and `prelude-parity-test` ran on Node |\n172: | `bb test:cljd` | not run, per the brief |\n173: \n174: **New tests:**\n175: - `test/yang/python/antlr/e2e_c1_test.clj`: 13 deftests, each on all four VMs (ast-walker, semantic, stack, register) over the real modules. They cover every exit through `finally`, finally nesting and override, raise inside finally and handler-stack consistency, `with`, tuples and unpacking, slices, tuple value semantics and unhashable keys, arithmetic operators, membership and augmented assignment, comprehensions, comprehension scope, keyword arguments, and the P3 runtime items.\n176: - `lower_test.clj`, new goldens: try/finally, with, unpacking, comprehension, keyword call, and slice plus `**` regrouping. Refusal and syntax lists were rewritten: 15 refusals and 15 static errors. A parser-level test pins the `0755` rejection.\n177: - `scope_test.clj`: tuple, star and with targets; comprehension scopes, including where a first-iterable lambda belongs and a class-body comprehension.\n178: - `prelude_parity_test.cljc` (also runs on Node): 24 new cases for floor division, modulo, power, bitwise ops, tuple equality and keys, and slice positions on JS numbers.\n179: \n180: ## Mutation proof\n181: \n182: Each mutation temporarily broke the code that implements one item. I ran the related tests against it, then restored the code. A final run showed no mutation markers left and all 98 tests green.\n183: \n184: | # | Mutation | Tests that turned red |\n185: |---|---|---|\n186: | M1 | `py/unwind-to` stops running finally thunks (escape path) | finally-runs-on-every-exit, finally-nesting-and-override, with-statement: 12 failures across 4 VMs |\n187: | M1b | `py/raise` stops running finally thunks (raise path) | finally-runs-on-every-exit, raise-in-finally-and-handler-stack: 8 failures (`with` correctly unaffected, since its raise path is a handler) |\n188: | M1c | `py/with` ignores `__exit__`'s result | with-statement: 4 failures |\n189: | M2 | `py/key` stops normalizing tuples | tuples-and-unpacking, tuples-are-values-and-unhashable-lists: 8 failures |\n190: | M2b | `py/slice-bound` stops clamping the upper bound | **first run: slices-test still passed**, a real coverage gap. I added `t[1:100]`, `'hello'[-100:2]` and `t[100:0:-1]`, and it then failed 4/4. |\n191: | M3 | integer `//` drops the floor adjustment, and `**` regrouping is disabled | arithmetic-operators on 4 VMs gave `-7//2` → -3, `-7%3` → -1, `2**3**2` → 64, `-6&3` → 0; slice-and-power golden failed: 5 failures |\n192: | M4 | the first iterable is evaluated inside the comprehension scope | comprehension-scope: NameError on `n`, 4 failures |\n193: | M5 | the keyword duplicate-value check is removed | keyword-arguments: 4 failures |\n194: | M6 | the `globals` builtin branch is removed | gate-p3-runtime: NameError on `globals`, 4 failures |\n195: | M7 | repr always uses single quotes | gate-p3-runtime: 4 failures |\n196: \n197: ## Mechanical versus not\n198: \n199: **Mechanical:**\n200: - the new operator and comparison table entries;\n201: - the augmented forms;\n202: - the CST arms for `with`, slices, displays and tuple expressions;\n203: - the new builtins;\n204: - the rule reclassification.\n205: \n206: **Not mechanical:**\n207: 1. **finally and with.** The handler stack had to become typed, depth-numbered frames so that one data structure serves both `raise`, which unwinds until a handler, and escapes, which unwind to a captured depth. Getting \"return in finally overrides\" and \"raise in finally replaces\" right came from popping a frame before running its thunk, not from anything in the grammar. Continuations carry the control flow as the owner said, but the unwinding protocol around them was design work.\n208: 2. **Integer arithmetic.** The host offers only `+ - * / <`, and `/` returns a Ratio on the JVM but a double on JS. Floor division, modulo, power, bitwise ops and float floor had to become algorithms that give identical results on all hosts.\n209: 3. **Comprehension scope.** Scope analysis needed a new scope kind with a split rule: the first iterable belongs to the enclosing scope, while everything else, including nested lambdas, belongs to the comprehension.\n210: 4. **Keyword binding.** CPython's argument-binding algorithm as prelude code, with every failure a TypeError, is the largest single piece.\n211: 5. **Grammar mismatches the mapping had to correct.** The grammar groups `**` to the left, so the lowering regroups it. The grammar also accepts a lone `*a` that Python rejects.\n212: \n213: ## Unresolved concerns\n214: \n215: - **Error messages carry no counts.** \"g() takes too many positional arguments\" stands in for CPython's \"takes 1 positional argument but 2 were given\", because `data` has no number-to-string primitive.\n216: - **Set iteration and display order is insertion order,** which is deterministic but differs from CPython's hash order (for example, `{1, 0}` versus `{0, 1}`). The comprehension test avoids depending on the difference.\n217: - **`dict.items()`, `keys()` and `values()` return list snapshots,** not live views.\n218: - **`**` with a non-integral exponent raises NotImplementedError,** because there is no portable `pow`/`exp`. JVM long overflow in `**` is still a host error; big ints are a later slice.\n219: - **Escapes now pay an unwinding check.** Every `py/call-ec` escape runs `py/unwind-to`, which costs one depth comparison when no frames lie above the escape's depth. `try`/`finally` allocates one frame vector per entry, and every `py/try` and `py/call-ec` allocates one flag cell, as in phase B. Heap reclamation (60b60898) now collects these.\n220: - **The `(x) = 1` target is still rejected** as an unsupported assignment target. Python accepts it; it is rare.\n221: - **CLJD is verified by reading only.** The one new reader conditional, `code-unit` in `render.cljc`, puts `:cljd` first and uses `.codeUnitAt`. No protocol methods were added.\n222: - **cljstyle has not been run** on these edits."}],"deferred":false}}
