saved at depth k; unwind-to k pops frames with depth > k. During raise-driven unwinding, finally frames were pushed with depth computed from chain at that moment. All consistent because chain is linear.\n\nOne risk: py/call-ec's escape does `(py/unwind-to (py/frame-depth saved))` then `(cell/set! handlers saved)`. Between the escape capture and invocation, if the same depth number gets reused by different frames (e.g., escape captured when stack depth was 2; later, at time of invocation, stack has a depth-3 frame from another try — unwind-to 2 pops them, running their finally!). Wait — is that correct? If the escape is invoked while dynamic extent has extra frames on the stack — that means the escape was called from a deeper dynamic context. When can that happen? Return escape invoked inside a finally thunk during unwinding? Consider:\n\n```python\ndef f():\n    try:\n        return g()      # return escape captured at depth d\n    finally:\n        h()             # h calls the escape again? no\n```\n\nMore realistically: escape invoked from deeper context:\n```python\ndef outer():\n    for x in xs:        # brk escape captured at loop's depth d\n        try:\n            ...\n        finally:\n            if cond: break   # break inside finally! Python rejects but we accept (see #2)\n```\nbreak inside finally during normal loop iteration: handlers at that moment = [...loop depth d, finally frame depth d+1]. break invokes brk escape: unwind-to d pops finally frame — runs its thunk again?? No wait — the finally thunk is currently executing (we're inside it via normal completion path of py/try-finally: `(fin :py/None)` after handlers restored to saved). In py/try-finally normal path: handlers set to saved BEFORE fin runs. So finally frame is already popped. break's unwind-to d sees handlers = saved (depth d) → `(<= (get hs 3) d)` → stops. OK good.\n\nVia raise path: py/raise pops finally frame, sets handlers to rest, then runs thunk. Also popped before running. unwin