e is ((a**b)**c): outer node [expr((a**b)) ** c]. power-operands: a = expr(a**b) with 3 children and second is ** → conj (power-operands a) c → operands [a b c] → fold right → pow(a, pow(b, c)). Correct.\n\nHmm, but `(= 3 (count (:children a)))` — a's :children is [expr(a) ** expr(b)] = 3. OK.\n\nDanger: regrouping when left operand is `(-a) ** b`? a = expr node of unary minus → children [- expr] = 2 → not regrouped. Fine.\n\nWhat about `a ** (b ** c)` with explicit parens: right child is atom → left-to-right fine; but parse: outer [expr(a) ** atom(...)]. power-operands: a is `name` expr? If a is a single-child expr → `(p/rule? a \"expr\")` with 3 children fails → [a b]. Then fold: pow(lower(a), lower(b)), and b = atom(parenthesized expr) lowered recursively → correct.\n\nWait — actually is there an issue: `power-operands` checks `(p/rule? a \"expr\")` — in ANTLR's CST packets, do single-child expr wrappers get collapsed? `kids ctx n` returns packet children. If `a` is a rule \"expr\" wrapping atom with one child, count=1 ≠ 3 → fine.\n\nBut `-a ** b`: unary applies to power: Python -(a**b). If grammar gives expr[- expr(a**b)] → correct. But if the grammar's unary alt is higher precedence than '**' (listed earlier), then `-a ** b` parses as ((-a) ** b) — wrong vs Python. In Python's grammar power: atom_expr ['**' factor]; factor: ('+'|'-'|'~') factor | power — so unary is lower precedence than **: -a**b = -(a**b). Need to check the pinned g4 file. Let me look at the antlr grammar directory.\n\n10. **display function for testlist**: single element without comma → just that element; `(x,)` → has comma → tuple. `a, b = ...` target: expr_stmt's target side is testlist_star_expr → assign-target → target-elements checks count>1 or comma. Good.\n\n`(x) = 1` — report says rejected. Python accepts. Minor.\n\n11. **lower-expr-stmt**: chained assignment `a = b = value`: value lowered once into tmp, assigned left-to-right to targets. Python evaluates RHS once and assigns to targets left-to-right. Correct.\n\n12. **lower-augmented**: `+=` on name: `(assign-name ctx nm (app* op (read-name ctx target nm) rhs))`. For lists, py/iadd mutates cell contents in place and returns the same cell; assign-name sets the cell to the same reference. Aliasing preserved. But for GLOBALS: `g_list += [1]` → py/global-set(%globals, name, py/iadd(...)) — in-place mutation, then re-set. Fine.\n\nBut attribute/subscript augmented: `o.x += 1` → get o once, k once, setitem(o,k, op(getitem)). Python's semantics for subscript is get→op→set, correct. But `a[i] += x` should not re-evaluate `a` and `i` — they use let1 o, k. Good.\n\nWait — Python subtlety: `a[i] += x` catches exception from getitem? No, for lists (not get/set via __getitem__ with in-place semantics and exceptions)... irrelevant subset.\n\nBut augmented to a name: reads via read-name; unbound local → UnboundLocalError via py/local-get. Python: `x += 1` with unbound x → UnboundLocalError. Correct.\n\n13. **subscript-key for tuple keys**: `d[1,2]` → py/tuple of keys; getitem normalizes key. Good. `d[1:2, 3]` → multi-dim slice rejected. Good.\n\n14. **slice-parts**: `[a:b:c]` — parses children of subscript_: test?, ':' test?, sliceop?. What about `[a:b:c:d]`? Grammar doesn't allow. `[:]:` fine. But bug: stop is `(first (filter #(p/rule? % \"test\") after))` where after = children after the first ':'. But sliceop contains `':' test?` — sliceop's test is a child of sliceop not of subscript_, so filter on `after` (direct children) finds only stop. OK. But wait — `split-with #(not (p/token? % \":\")) sk` — what about `[::2]`? sk = [: : 2]? First ':' at index 0 → before=[], after = [: 2]... `(rest after)` drops the first colon → [2]? Hmm: split-with returns before=[] after=[':' ':' 2]? No — sk's children are [':', ':', sliceop?]. Grammar's subscript_: `test? ':' test? sliceop?` with sliceop: `':' test?`. For `[::2]`: children are ':' ':' sliceop? Actually parse: test absent, ':', test absent, sliceop(':' 2). So sk = [':' sliceop]. split-with → before=[] after=[':' sliceop]; after=(rest)= [sliceop]. stop = first test rule in after → sliceop is a rule not a \"test\" → nil. sliceop found → step = its test. start is nil. Good.\n\nFor `[a:b]`: sk=[test ':' test] → before=[test], after=[':' test] → rest → [test] → stop. Good.\n\nBut `(when (some #(p/token? % \":\") sk) ...)` else nil → plain index. `d[x]` → lower first kids. Good.\n\nEdge: subscript_ for step with `[a:b:c:d]` invalid in grammar. OK.\n\nEdge: `x[1]` with subscriptlist having a single subscript_ and no commas → lower-subscript. `x[1,]` — has comma → tuple. Good.\n\n15. **lower-classdef**: bases: `(rules ctx arglist \"argument\")` — but keyword args to class (metaclass=...) → count>1? `class C(metaclass=M)` → one argument → checks ak: `(= 1 (count ak)) (p/rule? (first ak) \"test\")` — a metaclass keyword argument is not a test rule → unsupported \"class keyword arguments\". Good. But `class C(Base1, Base2)` → multiple inheritance rejected. `class C(*bases)`? argument with '*' → ak's first is token '*' → not test → unsupported. Fine.\n\nBase defaults to `(u/v 'py.b/object)` — the PRELUDE's object (store key), not the module dict's object. This is P3(a) treatment: matches CPython (implicit base is builtins.object regardless of module binding). Verified claim: in CPython, `object = 1; class C: pass` — does C still get created with builtins.object as base? Yes: `__build_class__` with no bases uses type's default base builtins.object — the compiler doesn't reference the module namespace. Correct treatment.\n\n16. **except clause matching**: `(app* 'py/isinstance (u/v exc) (lower cls-node))`. cls-node could be a tuple of classes `except (A, B):` — py/isinstance with a tuple cls → py/subclass? compares class cell against tuple map → `= c target` false, recurses on base... eventually :py/None → false. So `except (A, B)` silently never matches! Python: tuple in except matches any of them. Is tuple supported? cls-node is \"te