c: `(p/token? k \"*\")` with second not a param-rule → bare star → `(recur (rest ks) :kwonly spec)` — star? not set. And bind-args: with-star only if star. So code vector layout = positional, kwonly..., kwstar dict — and all-params (cells) = named params in written order = positional, kwonly, kwstar name. Consistent.\n\nFor `*args` case: star? true; all-params includes \"args\" in written position. bind-args: with-star = conj slots tuple → after positional, before kwonly. Written order: positional, *args, kwonly. Consistent. Good.\n\n35. **Duplicate parameter check**: `(when-not (= (count all-params) (count (distinct all-params))) (syntax! ...))` — good, \"duplicate argument in function definition\" matches CPython's message.\n\n36. **lower-comprehension's elems**: `(filterv #(not (p/rule? % \"comp_for\")) (rule-kids ctx comp-node))` — for dict comp: key and value (2 elements). For testlist_comp: elements before comp_for. But `filter p/rule?` excludes tokens (commas). For list comp `[x for ...]`: elems = [test(x)] → emit uses first. Good. Dict: dictorsetmaker children [test ':' test comp_for] → elems = [k v]. Good.\n\nHmm wait — for list comp, elements are \"test\" rules? testlist_comp: test (comp_for|...) — children are rules and tokens. filterv p/rule? keeps rule children. But star_expr check → syntax error \"iterable unpacking cannot be used in comprehension\" — Python: `[*x for x in y]` SyntaxError \"iterable unpacking cannot be used in comprehension\". Message matches.\n\n37. **Set/dict comp emits**: py/set-add / py/dict-set. Dict comp duplicate keys → overwrite, insertion order keeps first position — matches Python.\n\n38. **comprehension locals**: cells for `(:locals s)` — comprehension scope's locals = its for targets (assigned). resolve in cctx → :cell for targets. But — lambda inside comprehension capturing target: same cell. `[lambda: i for i in range(3)]` → all closures share one cell → 2,2,2. Correct Python 3 semantics (i's cell is single, comprehension executes eagerly, so value is last one... wait actually in Python: `fs = [lambda: i for i in range(3)]; [f() for f in fs]` → [2,2,2]. Yes correct.\n\n39. **scope.cljc's resolve for :nonlocal in comprehension**: enclosing-binding walks parents, skipping class scopes. Correct Python (comprehension's nonlocal... edge).\n\nBut: `:class` resolve → locals → :class-attr. Method body inside class: function scope; resolve a name bound in the class body from inside a method → enclosing-binding skips :class → global. Correct Python!\n\n40. **read-name for :class-attr uses `(u/v (:class ctx))`** — the cls's let symbol. Inside class body ctx :class is set. Method body has :class nil, so resolve doesn't yield :class-attr there (function scope walks past class). But wait — resolve happens in the lowering scope: inside method body, scope = function scope; resolve skips class → never :class-attr. Good.\n\n41. **render.cljc** — need to read repr fixes.\n\n42. **py/getattr on :class returns `__name__` fallback** — classes have a __name__ attribute. Note: py/make-class doesn't store __name__ in attrs; getattr synthesizes. Setting `C.__name__ = x`? setattr stores in attrs; then getattr finds in attrs first (class-lookup before __name__ fallback). Good.\n\n43. **py/getattr on :instance**: own attrs, then class-lookup, binding functions as methods. Data descriptors not modeled (fine for subset).\n\n44. **py/with gets __exit__ then __enter__ via py/getattr mgr — on instance, py/bind gives bound method. py/call exit [type value tb] → bound method prepends self. Good.\n\n45. **py/type-of e** — for exception e instance → its class cell. __exit__ receives class. Good.\n\n46. Now — **handler stack depth on re-entry of py/try inside finally thunk**: thunk runs with handlers = rest (frame popped). If thunk has its own try, pushes on rest. Depth numbers: py/frame computes depth = 1 + depth(rest). Saved depths are consistent. unwind-to d compares `(get hs 3)` — depth of top frame. Escape captured saved at depth k; unwind-to k pops frames with depth > k. During raise-driven unwinding, finally frames were pushed with depth computed from chain at that moment. All consistent because chain is linear.\n\nOne risk: py/call-ec's escape does `(py/unwind-to (py/frame-depth saved))` then `(cell/set! handlers saved)`. Between the escape capture and invocation, if the same depth number gets reused by different frames (e.g., escape captured when stack depth was 2; later, at time of invocation, stack has a depth-3 frame from another try — unwind-to 2 pops them, running their finally!). Wait — is that correct? If the escape is invoked while dynamic extent has extra frames on the stack — that means the escape was called from a deeper dynamic context. When can that happen? Return escape invoked inside a finally thunk during unwinding? Consider:\n\n```python\ndef f():\n    try:\n        return g()      # return escape captured at depth d\n    finally:\n        h()             # h calls the escape again? no\n```\n\nMore realistically: escape invoked from deeper context:\n```python\ndef outer():\n    for x in xs:        # brk escape captured at loop's depth d\n        try:\n            ...\n        finally:\n            if cond: break   # break inside finally! Python rejects but we accept (see #2)\n```\nbreak inside finally during normal loop iteration: handlers at that moment = [...loop depth d, finally frame depth d+1]. break invokes brk escape: unwind-to d pops finally frame — runs its thunk again?? No wait — the finally thunk is currently executing (we're inside it via normal completion path of py/try-finally: `(fin :py/None)` after handlers restored to saved). In py/try-finally normal path: handlers set to saved BEFORE fin runs. So finally frame is already popped. break's unwind-to d sees handlers = saved (depth d) → `(<= (get hs 3) d)` → stops. OK good.\n\nVia raise path: py/raise pops finally frame, sets handlers to rest, then runs thunk. Also popped before running. unwind-to similarly pops before running. So thunks always run with their own frame popped. An escape inside a thunk unwinds to its own saved depth — frames above that get popped and their thunks run — but the currently executing thunk's frame is already popped. However — re-entrancy: if thunk T is running (frame popped) and contains an escape that triggers unwind-to running thunk T's frame again... not possible, frame already popped.\n\nBut one real danger: escape invoked while stack contains frames of the callee's context:\n```python\ndef f():\n    try:\n        return 1     # escape captured with saved = stack at f body start, depth d\n    finally:\n        ...\n```\nEscape invoked in py/call-ec's `r x` — direct. Fine.\n\nWhat about break escape invoked from inside a function called in the loop body?\n```python\ndef helper(brk): brk(None)   # can't — brk isn't first-class in Python\n```\nPython doesn't expose escapes. OK — all escapes are lexically scoped within loop/function. Invocation always happens within the dynamic extent where intervening frames have depths > escape depth and are genuinely pending (i.e., we're inside those try/finally bodies). So unwind-to is correct.\n\nActually one more: **return captured at function entry (saved = stack at py/call-ec time = function's caller's dynamic context!)**. py/call-ec in lower-funcdef: saved = handlers when the function was called — could include caller's try frames (if function called inside caller's try block). return escape does unwind-to(depth saved) — pops frames pushed after call — i.e., function's own try/finally frames — and runs their finally. Correct: return inside a function runs that function's pending finally but not caller's (caller's frames have depth ≤ saved). Correct!\n\nAnd exception propagation across function boundaries: py/raise walks the global stack including caller's frames — a raise inside callee propagates to caller's handler, running callee's and caller's finally frames along the way. Correct Python semantics.\n\n47. **py/try's `handler` runs after handlers restored** — if handler code raises, propagates outward, skipping... Python: exception inside except block propagates (with __context__). Fine.\n\n48. **Interaction: with inside try, exception suppressed, else runs** — fine.\n\n49. **py/with's `hit` flag** — but if suite raises, handler runs exit, suppresses → returns None; then py/try's normal completion → orelse (None) → then outer py/try-finally normal completion → fin(None) → hit true → skip. Good.\n\nBut subtle: if exception suppressed, Python continues after with statement. Here py/with returns None and lowering continues. Good.\n\nIf handler's `py/raise e` (non-suppressed): raise walks from handlers = ... wait, during handler execution, handlers = saved (py/try restored). py/with's try-finally frame is above saved? Let me trace: py/with calls py/try-finally(body=..., fin=...). py/try-finally pushes :finally frame F1 (handlers = saved_outer + F1). Body calls py/try which pushes handler frame H (saved_inner = stack with F1). Body raises → py/raise pops... stack = [H, F1, ...]: top H is :handler → deliver to H's continuation → py/try re-entry: handlers := saved_inner (= [F1, ...]) then handler runs → hit=true, exit called, returns falsy → py/raise e again → now stack top = F1 (:finally) → pop F1, run thunk with e → hit true → skip exit call → then recurse (py/raise e) → continue outward. Correct: __exit__ called once with exception info.\n\n50. **Determinism**: gensym nowhere; gen uses node id. `contains-rule?` is deterministic. `filter p/rule?` on vectors. In scope: `(sort-by key scopes)` on numeric ids. global-names set — membership only. prelude: dict's :index is a host map — used for lookup only, never iterated... let me verify: py/kw-extend uses :keys/:vals vectors. py/snapshot-obj for dict uses :keys/:vals. py/dict-items uses keys/vals. OK — no host map iteration. But wait: `(into (map (fn [[nm key _]] [nm key])) builtin-classes)` inside builtin-names — host map construction at compile time, fine (data not iteration order — into {} from vector... order of builtin-names map is irrelevant, used via get/contains?).\n\nHmm — one more: lower.cljc's `unsupported-rules`, `handled-rules`, `consumed-rules` — compile-time maps/sets; tests iterate? \"every grammar rule is classified\" test probably iterates grammar rules and checks membership — set membership is fine.\n\nBut `(some #(p/token? % \"**\") parts)` — vector. Fine.\n\nIn prelude — py/bind-keywords iterates kwargs vector. py/kw-pairs iterates vectors. Good.\n\nAnywhere iterating a Clojure map in lowered code path? scope.cljc analyze: `(into (set (:assigned module)) (mapcat :globals (vals scopes)))` — vals of host map: order non-deterministic? vals of a Clojure persistent map are actually deterministic for the same map (hash order stable across same JVM/version, but might differ across hosts?). global-names is only used as a set → membership → order irrelevant. Fine.\n\ncheck-nonlocals: sorted by key — deterministic. doseq on sorted nonlocals — deterministic.\n\n51. **py/global-get's name argument**: `(u/lit {:py/str name})` — a host map as literal in datoms → lookup key equality on host maps — fine.\n\n52. **py/key numeric → double**: `(get (get c :index) n ...)` where index is keyed by doubles. But global dict keys are {:py/str \"x\"} maps — py/key returns map itself → index keyed by maps. Host map as key inside host map: equality by content — deterministic lookup. On JS host: clj maps fine.\n\nBut — **py/key for strings**: string keys in guest dict remain as maps {:py/str \"x\"}. py/dict-has? uses py/key — consistent. Good.\n\nWait, one more: py/key for :py/None? Returns k itself (content-type nil → k). None as dict key — works, equality by keyword. Good. bool keys: numeric → double. Good. Tuple keys nested → recursion. Good. Instance as key? py/content-type → :instance → not in list/dict/set check → returns cell reference itself → identity key. Python: default object hash = identity. Correct!\n\nFunction as key → cell reference → identity. Python: yes.\n\nBut — **str vs tuple containing str**: fine.\n\n**py/key collision: {:py/str \"x\"} as key vs normalized tuple-key map {:py/tuple-key [...]}** — distinguished. Good.\n\n53. **py/contains on range materializes** (py/to-vector c) — O(n) membership and memory; Python's range __contains__ is O(1). `x in range(10000000)` → 10M vector. Cost/DoS-ish. P3.\n\nAlso py/to-vector on a dict yields keys — `x in d` uses dict-has? directly, good.\n\n54. **py/range-len iterates** O(n) for len(range(...)) — Python is O(1). `len(range(10**9))` → billion loops → effectively hang. Reachable within literals ≤2^53: range(2**53)? A loop counting up one at a time — hang. Cost issue, P3.\n\n55. **py/ipow's recursion uses py/int-floordiv e 2** — int-floordiv calls divmod-pos with abs — fine, O(log e) depth each O(log) → fine.\n\n56. **py/pow float base negative exponent**: `(py/float (/ 1.0 (py/ipow x (- 0 e))))` — ipow with float base: `(* h h)` double — fine. 2.0**-1 = 0.5. Good.\n\nint ** negative: same path → float. Python: 2**-1 = 0.5 float. Good.\n\n57. **py/floor used in py/pow to check integrality of float exponent**: `(if (py/float? b) (< (py/floor y) y) false)` — py/floor on huge float raises OverflowError instead of NotImplementedError; NaN → py/floor NaN: comparisons `(if (if (< x 9007...) (> x -9007...) false)` — NaN both false → OverflowError. Python: 2**nan = nan. Deviation edge — but NaN can't even be written as a literal (float('nan') is not a builtin). Only via overflow: 1e308*10 → inf. py/floor inf → OverflowError. Python: 2**inf = inf. Edge deviation, ignorable/P3 at most.\n\n58. **int // 0 → ZeroDivisionError message \"integer division or modulo by zero\"** matches CPython for int; for float `7.0//0.0` CPython says \"float floor division by zero\"; here same message. Minor.\n\n59. **py/mod int**: int-mod uses int-floordiv — floor semantics → sign of divisor. -7 % 3 = 2. Good. 7 % -3 = -2: int-floordiv(7,-3): divmod-pos(7,3)=[2,1]; signs differ; r≠0 → -3. 7 - (-3 * -3) = 7-9 = -2. Correct.\n\n60. **py/floordiv float path**: `(py/float (py/floor (/ (* 1.0 a) b)))` — for int/float mix. `7 // 2.0` = 3.0. Good. But on JVM `(/ (* 1.0 7) 2)` → 3.5 double, floor → 3, py/float. Good. On JS same.\n\nBut: **`(/ ...)` on JVM can produce Ratio** if both args int — they do `(* 1.0 (py/num a))` first → double. Good, avoids Ratio. But wait — py/floor receives a double on all hosts. py/floor-descend uses `(/ p 2)` — on JVM p is... p starts as `(/ (py/pow2-above y 1) 2)` — pow2-above returns int (long) → on JVM `(/ long 2)` → Ratio when odd! e.g., (/ 1 2) → 1/2 Ratio. Then floor-descend comparisons with Ratio work on JVM (numeric tower), and `(/ p 2)` keeps ratio exact. On JS `(/ 1 2)` → 0.5 double. Different types but numerically equivalent results? On JVM Ratio is exact — binary descent with exact halving down to 1/2^k until p < 1 → terminates when p < 1: on JVM (/ 1/2 2) = 1/4 ... never below 1?? `(< p 1)`: 1/2 < 1 true → terminates. Fine — p halves: 2^k ... 1 → next (/ 1 2) = 1/2 < 1 stop. Same steps on JS. Equivalent. But does `(+ n p)` on JVM with Ratio n? n starts at 0 (long), p Ratio → sum Ratio; comparisons fine. Result n at termination: integer-valued Ratio? `(<= (+ n p) x)` — n accumulates by adding p values (halves) — n could be Ratio like 3/2? No — n only increases by p when (+ n p) ≤ x, x is double... n could become fractional (e.g., x=2.7: p descent 2→ n=2, p=1: 3>2.7 no; p=1/2: 2.5≤2.7 → n=2.5; p=1/4: 2.75>2.7 no; p=1/8: 2.625≤2.7 → n=2.625... terminates with n=2.6875? eventually p<1... wait, stop condition `(< p 1)` — on JVM p goes 2,1,1/2,1/4,... and stops when p<1, i.e., p=1/2 → returns n after adding granularity 1?? Hmm: floor-descend x n p: if p<1 → n. Steps: p starts at (pow2-above)/2 ≥ 1... let me trace x=2.7: pow2-above 2.7 → k: 1→2→4 (4>2.7) returns 4. p=2. floor-descend(2.7, 0, 2): p≥1: 0+2≤2.7 → n=2, p=1. (2.7,2,1): 2+1=3>2.7 → n=2, p=1/2. Now p<1 → return 2. Correct floor=2. So granularity stops at 1 — n always ends up integer-valued (only adds p when p≥1). On JVM n could be long + ... only adds p values ≥1 → n integer. Good, exact.\n\nBut precision: x is a double within ±2^53; p doubles from 1 — exact in both. Fine.\n\n61. **py/divmod-pos**: recursion `(py/divmod-pos a (+ b b))` — depth log2(a/b). Each level doubles b. Terminates when b > a. Fine. But intermediate `(+ b b)` overflow if b near 2^63 on JVM → throws. Within 2^53 doc, fine.\n\n62. **py/bit-op**: recursion depth = bits until both operands 0/-1. For values within 2^53 → ≤54 levels. int-floordiv/int-mod each call divmod-pos — per-bit O(log) cost — fine.\n\nWait — py/bit1 for :xor `(if (= x y) 0 1)`; for bits x,y ∈ {0,1}. But py/int-mod a 2 for negative a: int-mod(-3,2) = 1 (Python). Good — floor semantics gives non-negative bits. And int-floordiv(-3,2) = -2. Recursion: -3 & 3: bits: mod(-3,2)=1, mod(3,2)=1 → and=1; recurse (-2, 1): mod(-2,2)=0, mod(1,2)=1 → 0; recurse (-1, 0): a=-1,b=0 both in {0,-1} → -(bit1(and, 1, 0)) = -0 = 0. Total: 1 + 2*0 + 4*0 = 1. -3&3 = 1. Correct (Python: -3 & 3 = 1).\n\n-6 & 3: mod(-6,2)=0 → bit 0; recurse(-3,1): mod(-3,2)=1, mod(1,2)=1 → 1*2=2; recurse(-2,0): both: -2 not in {0,-1}... a=-2,b=0: b=0 but a=-2 → not both → recurse: bit1(mod(-2,2)=0, mod(0,2)=0)=0; recurse(-1, 0) → both terminal → -(bit1(and,1,0))=0. So -6&3 = 0+2+0 = 2. Python: -6&3 = 2. Correct. (Report M3 mutation shows broken version → 0; good version → 2.)\n\n63. **py/invert**: -x-1. Correct.\n\n64. **py/lshift**: a * 2^n. Python's int << n. Negative a: -1 << 3 = -8: * works. Good. Overflow past 2^53/2^63 diverges per host (issue noted earlier).\n\n65. **py/rshift**: int-floordiv a 2^n → arithmetic shift. Good.\n\n66. **Shift count type**: `1 << 2.0`? py/int? 2.0 → float → TypeError \"unsupported operand type for a shift\". Python: `1 << 2.0` → TypeError. Good. `1 << True` → int? true → 2^1. Python: True is int → 2. Good.\n\n67. **py/arith**: bool + bool → int; float tagging via py/float? checks. `True + True` = 2 int. Python is 2. Good. Float + int → py/float. Good.\n\n68. **py/compare across int/float**: compares nums. Good.\n\n69. **py/truthy on float NaN**: py/zero? NaN → (= NaN 0) false, (= NaN 0.0) false → not zero → truthy. Python: NaN is truthy. Good.\n\n70. **py/eq numeric path `(<= a b) (>= a b)`** — NaN == NaN → false. Python: nan == nan is False. Good. But NaN inside list eq → element-wise py/eq false → [nan] == [nan] is False; Python: [nan]==[nan] is True (identity fast-path!). Deviation edge, ignorable.\n\n71. **py/is**: `(= a b)` — for immutables: same type and value per ruling (owner decision). `1 is 1` → true; CPython also true (small int cache). `1000 is 1000` here true; CPython interactive false — but the language reference permits. Ruling approved. But: **`py/is` on tuples**: `(= a b)` host map equality — (1,2) is (1,2) → maps equal → true. Python: could be False (distinct objects). Ruling: immutables are same-type-and-value. Approved. But subtle: tuple containing cell references (a list inside a tuple — impossible: tuple elements could be list references!). `([1],) is ([1],)` → map equality recursively compares items; item = cell reference map {:type :cell-ref ...} → equality of reference data → same cell → equal. Two distinct lists with same contents → different references → not equal → is → false. Python also false. But `x=[1]; (x,) is (x,)` → both tuples contain same reference → host = true. Python: two separate tuple objects → False! Deviation: `is` on tuples of same contents returns true here but Python false. Ruling says immutables are same-type-and-value — tuples are immutable but values contain mutable references. Edge deviation P3.\n\n72. **Snapshot/print path** — render.cljc handles repr. Need to read.\n\n73. **py/snapshot-exc**: type name from class, args from \"args\" attribute. Unhandled exceptions surface as {:type :args}. Good.\n\n74. **py/run-module**: `(cell/set! py.rt/handlers nil)` — resets handler stack. But what if a prior module left frames (crashed mid-unwind)? py/try catches everything and unwinds, so... if handler raises during handling, frames could leak? py/run-module's py/try re-entry restores handlers to saved=nil... actually run-module's try's saved = nil (after reset). Unhandled → :py/no-handler effect — handlers stack state afterwards? Reset next run. Fine.\n\n75. **`(:py/no-handler e)`** — need to verify the vm handles this keyword application. Pre-existing from spike — tests pass, fine.\n\n76. **cost of py/for-each recursion** — deep recursion in guest? These are UAST lambdas executed by VM — py/collect etc recursion is guest-level recursion running as continuations on VM (heap, no stack overflow per mappability ruling \"continuations are heap data\"). OK.\n\nWait — are py/ functions executed as guest code? Yes, prelude is UAST run on VMs. Recursion via continuation = no host stack overflow (per ruling). But cost: list of 1M elements → 1M continuation frames — heap reclamation handles. OK.\n\n77. **`(u/lit {:py/str name})`** for global keys — a datom literal containing host map: encoder handles.\n\n78. **lower-atom string**: `(apply str (map #(parse-string n (:text %)) ks))` — ks are all children (STRING tokens); adjacent string concatenation. But wait — ks includes all children of atom; for a name atom, handled earlier. For STRING atoms, all children are STRING tokens. OK.\n\n79. **unescape**: `(= e \"x\") (recur (+ i 4) ...)` — `\\x` requires exactly 2 hex digits in Python; `(subs body (+ i 2) (min len (+ i 4)))` takes up to 2 chars and hex-value validates `[0-9a-fA-F]+` — but `\\xZZ`... if fewer than 2 hex digits available (e.g., `\"\\x4\"` at end), parse-radix with 1 digit \"4\" → 4 — Python: SyntaxError (truncated \\xXX). Accepts invalid program, minor P3. Also `\\x4g` → subs takes \"4g\" → re-matches fails → syntax error. OK. `\\u` similar (Python requires exactly 4). Minor.\n\nAlso octal escapes: up to 3 digits — Python allows \\0-3 digits. parse-radix digits \"0\"-\"7\" — but does Python cap octal escape value at 0o377? No — Python's string octal escapes allow up to \\377; larger like \\777 → ValueError? Actually Python: \"\\777\" → ValueError? octal escape value out of range? Python: escapes \\0-\\377 allowed; \"\\400\" → SyntaxError? Actually Python: \"\\777\" → error \"octal escape value \\777 exceeds 377\". Yes, SyntaxError/ValueError in Python 3? Emits SyntaxWarning... no — ValueError: octal escape value \\777 exceeds 377. Here parse-radix allows up to max-exact-int → \"\\777\" = 511 accepted. Deviation P3 (accepts invalid). hex-value only checks ≤0x10FFFF for \\U... wait, hex-value applied to all of x, u, U — checks cp > 0x10FFFF → syntax error \"illegal Unicode character in \\U escape\". For \\x, cp ≤ FF anyway. \\u with more than 4 digits? subs caps. OK.\n\nAlso **parse-radix on digits > max-exact-int → unsupported \"integer literal beyond 2^53\"** — even for hex escape values (fine).\n\n80. **0755 guard**: `(and (str/starts-with? t \"0\") (re-find #\"[1-9]\" t))` → syntax error. But `09` → error; `0_0`? t = \"0_0\" with underscores removed first → \"00\" → starts with 0 but re-find [1-9] nil → parse-radix \"00\" base 10 → 0. Python: \"00\" is valid (= 0)! Yes, `x = 00` in Python 3 → 0? Actually Python 3: `00` is allowed (zero can be repeated): `>>> 00` → 0. And `01` → SyntaxError. Here: \"01\" → starts with 0, has 1 → syntax error. Correct. \"00\" → 0 correct. But float \"0.5\"? re-find on t \"[.eE]\" first → float path before leading-zero check. Good. \"0e5\"? float path. Good. \"0x0\" handled. OK.\n\nWait — the order: float check `[.eE]` before leading-zero check. Python: \"0.5\" fine. Leading-zero check only reaches pure decimal digits. But hmm: \"0_1\"? t=\"01\" → syntax error. Python: 0_1 → SyntaxError? Python: `0_1` → 1? Let me think — underscores are allowed: `1_000` = 1000. `0_1` — I think it's valid = 1? Python tokenizer allows underscores between digits; leading zero rule: \"0_1\" → SyntaxError? Testing memory: Python 3 allows `0_0` = 0, and `0_1`... I recall `0_1` is SyntaxError(\"leading zeros in decimal integer literals are not permitted\"). Hmm, actually the rule is about non-zero digits after leading 0 — `0_1` strips to \"01\" → error. CPython: yes, `0_1` is a SyntaxError. Our impl: t = \"01\" → error. Correct!\n\n81. **parse-number float**: `{:py/float (Double/parseDouble t)}` — but py/float? checks `(not (nil? (get x :py/float)))` — if value is nil? Not possible. But if parsed value is 0.0: `{:py/float 0.0}` — get → 0.0, nil? false → OK. But py/str? check `(not (nil? (get x :py/str)))` for {:py/float 0.0} → get :py/str → nil → not nil? → false. OK.\n\nHmm wait — py/numeric? for {:py/float x}: `(py/float? x)` true → numeric. Then checks `(nil? (get x :py/str))` etc. Order: numeric? checks true/false first, then float?, then... fine.\n\nBut: **float -0.0, inf**: parse \"1e400\" → Double/parseDouble → Infinity. Python: 1e400 → inf float. Actually Python: `1e400` → inf. Same. JS parseFloat(\"1e400\") → Infinity. Dart double.parse → Infinity. Consistent.\n\n82. **lower-atom for NUMBER**: `(u/lit (parse-number ...))` — int → host long/double literal. Int ≤2^53 fits exactly in double on JS. Good.\n\nNow let me read render.cljc and the tests. Also let me check uast.cljc's u/lam, u/let1, mark-tails... and confirm `(get spec :kwonly)` nil path with data/count nil — let me grep data/count impl.\n\nAlso check e2e_c1_test.clj and lower_test.clj updates (rule classification tests), scope_test, prelude_parity_test.\n\nLet me read render.cljc first.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Prelude, lowering, and scope are read. Now render.cljc and the data module's `count` ("}]}}
