saved at depth k; unwind-to k pops frames with depth > k. During raise-driven unwinding, finally frames were pushed with depth computed from chain at that moment. All consistent because chain is linear.\n\nOne risk: py/call-ec's escape does `(py/unwind-to (py/frame-depth saved))` then `(cell/set! handlers saved)`. Between the escape capture and invocation, if the same depth number gets reused by different frames (e.g., escape captured when stack depth was 2; later, at time of invocation, stack has a depth-3 frame from another try — unwind-to 2 pops them, running their finally!). Wait — is that correct? If the escape is invoked while dynamic extent has extra frames on the stack — that means the escape was called from a deeper dynamic context. When can that happen? Return escape invoked inside a finally thunk during unwinding? Consider:\n\n```python\ndef f():\n    try:\n        return g()      # return escape captured at depth d\n    finally:\n        h()             # h calls the escape again? no\n```\n\nMore realistically: escape invoked from deeper context:\n```python\ndef outer():\n    for x in xs:        # brk escape captured at loop's depth d\n        try:\n            ...\n        finally:\n            if cond: break   # break inside finally! Python rejects but we accept (see #2)\n```\nbreak inside finally during normal loop iteration: handlers at that moment = [...loop depth d, finally frame depth d+1]. break invokes brk escape: unwind-to d pops finally frame — runs its thunk again?? No wait — the finally thunk is currently executing (we're inside it via normal completion path of py/try-finally: `(fin :py/None)` after handlers restored to saved). In py/try-finally normal path: handlers set to saved BEFORE fin runs. So finally frame is already popped. break's unwind-to d sees handlers = saved (depth d) → `(<= (get hs 3) d)` → stops. OK good.\n\nVia raise path: py/raise pops finally frame, sets handlers to rest, then runs thunk. Also popped before running. unwind-to similarly pops before running. So thunks always run with their own frame popped. An escape inside a thunk unwinds to its own saved depth — frames above that get popped and their thunks run — but the currently executing thunk's frame is already popped. However — re-entrancy: if thunk T is running (frame popped) and contains an escape that triggers unwind-to running thunk T's frame again... not possible, frame already popped.\n\nBut one real danger: escape invoked while stack contains frames of the callee's context:\n```python\ndef f():\n    try:\n        return 1     # escape captured with saved = stack at f body start, depth d\n    finally:\n        ...\n```\nEscape invoked in py/call-ec's `r x` — direct. Fine.\n\nWhat about break escape invoked from inside a function called in the loop body?\n```python\ndef helper(brk): brk(None)   # can't — brk isn't first-class in Python\n```\nPython doesn't expose escapes. OK — all escapes are lexically scoped within loop/function. Invocation always happens within the dynamic extent where intervening frames have depths > escape depth and are genuinely pending (i.e., we're inside those try/finally bodies). So unwind-to is correct.\n\nActually one more: **return captured at function entry (saved = stack at py/call-ec time = function's caller's dynamic context!)**. py/call-ec in lower-funcdef: saved = handlers when the function was called — could include caller's try frames (if function called inside caller's try block). return escape does unwind-to(depth saved) — pops frames pushed after call — i.e., function's own try/finally frames — and runs their finally. Correct: return inside a function runs that function's pending finally but not caller's (caller's frames have depth ≤ saved). Correct!\n\nAnd exception propagation across function boundaries: py/raise walks the global stack including caller's frames — a raise inside callee propagates to caller's handler, running callee's and caller's finally frames along the way. Correct