k = [':' sliceop]. split-with → before=[] after=[':' sliceop]; after=(rest)= [sliceop]. stop = first test rule in after → sliceop is a rule not a \"test\" → nil. sliceop found → step = its test. start is nil. Good.\n\nFor `[a:b]`: sk=[test ':' test] → before=[test], after=[':' test] → rest → [test] → stop. Good.\n\nBut `(when (some #(p/token? % \":\") sk) ...)` else nil → plain index. `d[x]` → lower first kids. Good.\n\nEdge: subscript_ for step with `[a:b:c:d]` invalid in grammar. OK.\n\nEdge: `x[1]` with subscriptlist having a single subscript_ and no commas → lower-subscript. `x[1,]` — has comma → tuple. Good.\n\n15. **lower-classdef**: bases: `(rules ctx arglist \"argument\")` — but keyword args to class (metaclass=...) → count>1? `class C(metaclass=M)` → one argument → checks ak: `(= 1 (count ak)) (p/rule? (first ak) \"test\")` — a metaclass keyword argument is not a test rule → unsupported \"class keyword arguments\". Good. But `class C(Base1, Base2)` → multiple inheritance rejected. `class C(*bases)`? argument with '*' → ak's first is token '*' → not test → unsupported. Fine.\n\nBase defaults to `(u/v 'py.b/object)` — the PRELUDE's object (store key), not the module dict's object. This is P3(a) treatment: matches CPython (implicit base is builtins.object regardless of module binding). Verified claim: in CPython, `object = 1; class C: pass` — does C still get created with builtins.object as base? Yes: `__build_class__` with no bases uses type's default base builtins.object — the compiler doesn't reference the module namespace. Correct treatment.\n\n16. **except clause matching**: `(app* 'py/isinstance (u/v exc) (lower cls-node))`. cls-node could be a tuple of classes `except (A, B):` — py/isinstance with a tuple cls → py/subclass? compares class cell against tuple map → `= c target` false, recurses on base... eventually :py/None → false. So `except (A, B)` silently never matches! Python: tuple in except matches any of them. Is tuple supported? cls-node is \"te