ep the test files.\n\nAlso `except (A, B) as e:` — same issue.\n\nHmm wait — maybe tuple in except_clause: `(first (rules ctx clause \"test\"))` — for `except (A, B):`, the test rule wraps an atom with parenthesized testlist → lowers via display → py/tuple (2 elements + no comma? has comma) → tuple. So cls-node lowers to a tuple. Yes, bug — unless py/isinstance has tuple handling elsewhere. It doesn't (I read it above).\n\nLet me grep for \"except (\" in the tests to check coverage.\n\n17. **raise_stmt with `from`** — unsupported. Bare raise outside except → unsupported (\"bare raise outside except\") — Python: RuntimeError \"No active exception to reraise\". Rejection is fine (loudly).\n\n18. **py/as-exception accepting any cell** — `raise [1,2]` becomes a list as exception; then py/raise delivers it to the handler; py/isinstance on a list → content-type :list ≠ :instance → false → re-raise → no handler → :py/no-handler. So `raise [1,2]` doesn't produce a TypeError as Python would, but terminates the module with a weird exception snapshot. py/snapshot-exc on a list → py/snapshot → a list. Deviation P3: should TypeError \"exceptions must derive from BaseException\" when the cell content isn't an instance (py/content-type ≠ :instance) or doesn't subclass BaseException. Actually Python also requires subclassing BaseException: `raise object()` → TypeError. Here any instance is accepted. P3.\n\n19. **except handler doesn't delete `as` name after the block** (Python deletes at end of except block). Deviation, P3, probably pre-existing from spike. Python: `except E as e:` deletes e after the clause. Here e remains bound. Minor fidelity.\n\n20. **lower-funcdef wraps body in py/call-ec only if there's a return_stmt** — with stop=scope-rules (doesn't cross functions). Returns inside a comprehension inside a function? Comprehension isn't in scope-rules' stop set! contains-rule? stops at funcdef/lambdef/classdef but not comprehension — but return isn't syntactically allowed inside a comprehension anyway (grammar: comp uses test_nocond/lambdef_nocond, no return_stmt). But `return` inside a lambda inside a function — stop includes lambdef. OK. `return` inside a finally inside... fine, still within function.\n\nWait — actually a subtle point: `contains-rule? ctx block \"return_stmt\" scope-rules` — return_stmt can appear nested in try/with/loops — not stop rules → found. Good.\n\n21. **Function body with return under py/call-ec**: escapes unwinds finally frames via unwind-to → finally runs on return. Good.\n\n22. **while loop's else**: executes when the test becomes false; break escapes past with-break's call-ec → skips else. Correct: break escape's continuation r is captured at with-break's py/call-ec, wrapping the whole `(u/let1 w ...)` including else? Let's see: with-break wraps `node` = the let1 w loop-with-else. `(app* 'py/call-ec (u/lam [brk] node))` — brk escape jumps past node entirely → skips else. Correct Python (break skips else).\n\n23. **for loop**: it = py/iterable (display target's testlist) — for target `for a, b in ...`: `(first (rules ctx n \"testlist\"))` hmm, for_stmt grammar: `for exprlist in testlist : block`. it = py/iterable(display ctx testlist). display: single without comma → value. `for x in a, b:` → testlist with comma → tuple. Python: iterates over tuple (a,b). Correct.\n\nIterator is materialized once (py/iterable converts str to tuple; list iterated by index — mutation during iteration: Python's list iterator sees appends! `l=[1]; for x in l: l.append(...)` — py/iter-at reads live items via cell/get by index each step → sees appends like Python. Good. Dict iteration while mutating — Python raises RuntimeError; here sees live keys. Deviation edge, P3 at most.\n\n24. **for's else**: orelse runs on :py/stop. break skips. Correct.\n\n25. **continue inside for**: loop-body wraps in call-ec per iteration — cont escape jumps to end of body, then recurses with w i+1. Good. But note: per-iteration py/call-ec allocation + flag cell — cost noted in report.\n\n26. **lower-try-except**: dispatch = if isinstance(exc, cls) → handler else fallthrough... base case = `(app* 'py/raise (u/v exc))` re-raise. Good. Handler runs with handlers restored to saved (py/try's re-entry). else only runs on normal completion. Good.\n\nBut: bare `except:` → cls-node nil → unconditional handler. `(when (and (nil? cls-node) (> (count ck) 1)) (unsupported! clause \"except clause\"))` — this catches `except as e:`? Weird grammar. Fine.\n\n27. **py/try's orelse runs after handlers restored** — exceptions inside else are not caught by that try. Correct per Python.\n\nBut wait: py/try first pass: `(body) (cell/set! handlers saved) (orelse)`. If body raises → continuation delivered → re-entry branch restores handlers and runs handler. But if handler itself raises → propagates with handlers = saved. Correct.\n\nOne issue: in py/try first pass, after `(body)` completes, sets handlers to saved, then `(orelse)`. But what if body escaped (return/break) via call-ec? Escape's unwind-to pops handler frame (without running) and restores. py/try's capture continuation is... escape jumps past, never returns to py/try. handlers were already set to saved by the escape wrapper. Good.\n\n28. **flag cell and multi-pass**: py/try uses `flag` cell; if continuation is called twice (not possible in this semantics). OK.\n\n29. **py/raise when no handlers**: `(:py/no-handler e)` — UAST form: application of keyword :py/no-handler? Probably handled by vm as effect... Pre-existing from spike. OK.\n\n30. **module-level handler stack reset**: py/run-module sets handlers to nil at start. Good — but nested module runs? N/A.\n\n31. **Keyword argument lowering: `(u/lit nm)` as keyword pair name, value lowered with ctx. Order of evaluation: positional and keywords — the reduce builds py/conj chain in order, so args are evaluated left-to-right as written (mixing positional/keyword). Python evaluates args left-to-right. Good.\n\nBut: `f(*a, **