{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Grammar: ANTLR left-recursive expr where alternatives are arranged in order of descending precedence:\n1. atom_expr\n2. expr '**' expr (in ANTLR, left-associative by default! That's why regrouping is needed)\n3. ('+'|'-'|'~')+ expr — unary\n4. '*' '/' '%' '//'\n5. '+' '-'\n6. shifts\n7. &\n8. ^\n9. |\n\nIn other words, '**' has higher precedence than unary → `-2 ** 2` = -(2**2) ✓ matches Python. And ** is left-associative in the parse → the lowering side regroups it ✓.\n\nWait, there's one problem: Python's `**` right operand allows unary: `2 ** -1`. In this grammar, the right operand of `expr '**' expr` is an expr; `-1` parses as unary expr (alternative 3) — since ** has higher precedence than unary, does `2 ** -1` parse as expr('**', 2, expr('-',1))? In ANTLR left-recursive rules, the right operand of a precedence-p binary operator is parsed at precedence p (right-recursive descent uses the same precedence level for right associativity, or p+1... For left-associative operators, the right operand is parsed at the next higher precedence, i.e. alternatives at or above p). Alternative 3 (unary) is a primary-style alternative (prefix), available when parsing the right operand at any precedence. Yes — ANTLR handles precedence climbing, and the right operand of a left-associative operator at precedence level p recursively parses expr with minimum precedence p+1... The unary alternative is like a prefix atom and is always available. Therefore `2 ** -1` = expr ** expr(-1) ✓. The e2e test `2 ** -1` outputs 0.5 ✓ confirmed.\n\nHowever: `2 ** -1` — in Python this parses as 2 ** (-1). ✓.\n\nThen what about the unary chain `('-' | '~')+ expr`: `--x`, `-~x`. The unary arm of lower-expr handles multiple tokens: ks = [- - x]? The grammar produces one node with children [- - x]. `(p/token? (first ks))` arm: reduce from peek with rseq(pop) → apply inner - then outer -. ✓.\n\nMixed: `-2 ** 2`: '**' alternative (alternative 2) has higher precedence than unary (alternative 3). Parsing `-2 ** 2`: starts with '-' token → unary alternative 3 at precedence 3; its operand `expr` is parsed at minimum precedence 4 (alternatives with precedence ≥ 4?)... Actually in ANTLR the operand of a prefix alternative is parsed with the precedence of that alternative... For the unary in alternative 3, the operand parses alternatives 1..3 (higher or equal precedence?). ANTLR: in left-recursive rules, each alternative has a precedence; the primary operand of a prefix operator uses precedence = alternative's precedence - 1, i.e. can include lower alternatives... Honestly: the empirical evidence — the e2e test asserts `-2 ** 2` = -4 on all four VMs and it passes. That means the parse gives -(2**2), matching Python. ✓ Good.\n\nNow — the `('@')` alternative: `expr '@' expr` is in alternative 4 of the grammar. lower-expr binary arm: `(get binary-ops (:text op))` — \"@\" is not in binary-ops → unsupported \"operator @\" ✓ (report says @ is rejected).\n\nAlso `<>` comp_op → the comparison table has no \"<>\" → unsupported ✓.\n\nThe name rule includes `'_'` and `'match'` as names — the lexer treats these specially; name-of takes the text of the first child — is there a token child for '_'/'match'? The name rule has one token child; name-of = (:text (first kids)) ✓.\n\nNow let's check the `**` regrouping bug: `(= 3 (count (:children a)))` and `(p/token? (second (kids ctx a)) \"**\")`. For a chain `a ** b ** c`: left-associative parse → node ((a**b)**c): children [expr(a**b), '**', expr(c)]. power-operands on outer: a = expr(a**b) — 3 children, 2nd is '**' → conj (power-operands a) c → recursively [a b] → [a b c] ✓ → fold right: pow(a, pow(b,c)) ✓.\n\nBut: what if the left operand of ** is itself a parenthesized `**` expression: `(a ** b) ** c`? Python: this is explicitly (a**b)**c. Parse: the outer ** node's left child = atom_expr? `(a ** b)` is atom (paren testlist) → not a rule expr with 3 children → no regrouping → pow(atom, c) = (a**b)**c ✓ correct — the parentheses are preserved.\n\nTricky case: `a ** b ** c` where the regrouping condition checks `(p/rule? a \"expr\")`. But in the CST packet, is the left child directly a rule node \"expr\"? ANTLR's left recursion produces a context with expr child in the expr context. Yes.\n\nDangerous case: unary within a chain: `a ** -b ** c`? Python: a ** (-(b**c))? Actually Python: `-b ** c` = -(b**c); `a ** -b ** c` = a ** (-(b**c)). Parsing here: right operand of first ** is expr('-' ...) — hmm, and the left recursion chain... This gets hairy but the test only asserts `2**3**2`. Edge cases with unary inside a ** chain can be misgrouped. Example: `2 ** -3 ** 2`: Python = 2 ** (- (3**2)) = 2**-9 = 0.00195. Here: outer node [expr(2) ** expr(?)]. Right child: can `- 3 ** 2` be a single unary node [- expr(3**2)]? If so pow(2, neg(pow(3,2))) ✓. Or parse might be different. power-operands only regroups when the left child is a 3-child ** expr — right child is not regrouped — right fold is correct for right associativity.\n\nWhat about regrouping when left child is `a ** b` and b is unary etc? — no problem.\n\nOne more: `power-operands` uses `(:children a)` but uses `kids ctx a` for tokens — consistent.\n\nNext, lower-expr binary arm `(let [[a op b] ks] ...)` — destructuring binds exactly 3; if ks has more than 3 children (a flat n-ary node like `a + b + c` in left-recursive parse is nested binary nodes, each with 3 children) ✓ left recursion nests. OK.\n\nNext: verify refusal/syntax list and rule classification test updates in lower_test.clj; verify comprehension scope cases in scope_test; new cases in prelude_parity_test; changes in e2e_test (host-registry / run-python helpers); lower_portable_test.\n\nAlso verify the with_stmt grammar and try_stmt grammar (grammar that allows break inside finally). And `for_stmt` uses \"testlist\" for iterable — grammar: `for_stmt: 'for' exprlist 'in' testlist ':' ...`. In lower-for, `(display ctx (first (rules ctx n \"testlist\")))` — display: single without comma → value ✓.\n\nAlso check the e2e helper (run-python) — modified e2e_test.clj. Let's quickly read the test files. lower_test.clj is probably big. Let's grep for the relevant parts.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Grammar confirmed: `**` (alt 2) outranks unary (alt 3), and left-associativity is corrected by the lowering's regrouping."}]}}
