{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Tests look good. Notes:\n\n- `x = a @ b` rejection: \"operator @\" — grammar alternative 4 contains '@', lower-expr's binary arm rejects ✓. `x @= b` \"augmented @=\" ✓.\n- Rule classification: `(set (seq Python3Parser/ruleNames))` = union of handled/consumed/unsupported, pairwise disjoint ✓.\n- Interesting rejection: `f(i for i in y)` — \"argument\" rule genexp → unsupported ✓ (f is not a known consuming builtin… wait, `f` is unresolvable → builtin? false → callee nil → not in consuming-builtins → unsupported ✓).\n- Static errors cover duplicate keywords, positional after keyword, bare *, multiple stars, etc.\n\nThings not covered by tests: `except (A, B):` — confirmed no coverage anywhere (grep found no \"except (\" within tests). Let me verify whether this bug actually exists: lower-try-except's `(app* 'py/isinstance (u/v exc) (lower cls-node))`. cls-node = `(first (rules ctx clause \"test\"))` — for `except (A, B):`, the test rule wraps an atom parenthesized testlist_comp containing commas → lower → display → py/tuple. py/isinstance(o, tuple) → py/subclass?(class, tuple-map) → never equal → false → falls through → re-raises. In Python it would catch either. So `except (A, B):` silently fails to catch. This is a P1/P2 issue — silent misbehavior on a core try-statement form listed in the language reference. try/except is in scope (Phase B had except; C1 added finally). The brief says \"rejected constructs fail loudly\" — but this isn't rejected, it's silently wrong. Since try/except with tuple is a core Python construct supported from Phase B (pre-existing issue?) — wait, was it in the Phase B spike? The except clause was in the spike. Did the spike claim to support `except (A,B)`? Probably it had the same py/isinstance issue. Pre-existing issue, but C1's brief item 1 explicitly covers try/except/finally semantics (\"handler stack integrity across nested finally/try/loops; tests for each exit kind and nesting\"), and the gate brief says \"finally on all exits (…including nesting…)\". Tuple except is not explicitly named in the gate checklist, but \"check Python 3 semantics on the claimed subset\" — the claimed subset includes try/except. Silently wrong semantics = P1 by the criteria I used on earlier gates? On the r2 gate P1 was for builtin shadowing (silently wrong value read). A silently uncaught exception changes program behavior: instead of executing the handler, the module dies with an unhandled exception snapshot. Loud at module level (exception appears in output) but wrong. Should we call it P2 (silent semantics divergence, but manifesting as a visible unhandled exception, construct not claimed in the report; report doesn't claim tuple except)? Hmm. Actually wait — let me re-check py/isinstance… Also builtin `isinstance(x, (A,B))` → false where Python returns true. Same root cause.\n\nActually let me check more carefully: is there a chance the except_clause's \"test\" for `(A, B)` lowers differently? except_clause grammar: `'except' (test ('as' NAME)?)? ':'`. test → atom '(' testlist_comp ')' → testlist_comp containing commas → display → py/tuple [A B]. Yes.\n\nSo finding 1 (P2): tuple exception classes in `except` and `isinstance` never match — silent divergence; fix: have py/isinstance iterate tuple classes (checking any), or statically reject tuple in except clause with a limited diagnostic. Python reference: except clause accepts tuples. Given the \"straightforward mapping\" directive and loud-failure invariant, silently never matching is a violation.\n\nSeverity call: gate brief lists P0-P3. Silent misbehavior on a construct that is accepted but not correctly implemented → I'll rule P1 if it's within the claimed subset. Is tuple except claimed? Report's \"currently supported\" item 1 says try/except/else/finally — doesn't enumerate tuple form. Not in rejection list either. Lowering accepts it. So: accepted construct, silently wrong. This is the \"fail loudly\" invariant violation. Should I say **P1**? Let me think about actual impact: `except (A, B):` is extremely common in real Python. When it doesn't catch, the exception propagates — program output shows an exception rather than wrong values. Divergence is observable but not silently wrong data… however handler side effects are also skipped. On previous gate r1, P1 was assigned to builtin-shadowing bug that yielded wrong output value. This time: unhandled exception surfaces (fails semi-loudly at runtime, wrong per Python). Also `isinstance(x, (A,B))` silently returns False — that is silent wrong data (e.g. `if isinstance(x, (int,float))` takes wrong branch, no error). That's a truly silent misbehavior on a supported builtin. I'll rate P1… but is isinstance with tuple \"claimed\"? isinstance is a supported builtin from the spike; the mappability ruling says subset boundary. Hmm. C1 brief says \"everything else remains rejected with limited diagnostic.\" isinstance(x, tuple-of-classes) is neither rejected nor correct. Rating P1 for the combination (silent wrong boolean from supported builtin + never-catching except clause), with concrete fix suggestion.\n\nWait — one more consideration: does `py/subclass?` handle target being a tuple at all… No. And also `except (A, B) as e` — same.\n\nAlso let me check that `raise` accepts tuple? `raise (A, B)` — as-exception: cell? no (tuple map) → content-type nil → type-error \"exceptions must derive from BaseException\" — Python: TypeError \"exceptions must derive from BaseException\" ✓ coincidentally fine.\n\nSecond area: **break/continue/return inside finally**. Python 3.8+: SyntaxError for break/continue inside finally. Here: accepted, and behaves like… break inside finally during normal completion: py/try-finally executes fin on normal path after restoring handler — the break escape jumps to loop exit. In Python it's a compile error. Acceptance of an invalid program — deviation, P3 (rare, and behavior is arguably reasonable).\n\nAlso `return` inside finally is legal in Python ✓ supported and tested (h() returns 2).\n\nThird: **continue inside except with loop** etc — fine.\n\nFourth: **deletion of except-as name**: Python deletes `e` after except block; here it remains bound. P3 deviation.\n\nFifth: **__exit__ lookup order**: py/with does getattr __exit__ then __enter__ ✓ matches reference (both are type-based lookups in CPython — here it's instance getattr with class lookup fallback; instance attribute `__exit__` would be found — Python looks up on type for implicit invocation of special methods (dunder), skipping instance attrs! Deviation: `mgr.__exit__ = f` as instance attr is used here but Python uses type's. Edge case, P3.)\n\nSixth: **with ... as target unpack failure** — assignment inside suite → __exit__ called with exception ✓ matches Python.\n\nSeventh: **py/with calls __exit__ with (type, value, None)** — Python passes traceback as third. Here None. Report notes this explicitly. Fine subset-wise (tracebacks out of scope).\n\nEighth: **genexp acceptance check**: `callee` is non-nil only for first trailer. But bug check — `sum(x for x in y)` — atom_expr children: atom(name sum), trailer(call). callee = \"sum\" ✓. But `x = (i for i in y)` in parens — atom path: `(scope/comprehension? ...)` → unsupported \"generator expression (phase C2)\" ✓ tested.\n\nBut: **`list(x for x in y)` where `list` is shadowed later in module**: builtin? uses whole-module assigned global-names → declared? true → callee nil → reject. Conservative ✓.\n\n**Method-call genexp**: `obj.m(x for x in y)` → callee nil (trailer i=0 is '('? wait — trailers: `.` then `(`. atom head is name \"obj\"; callee-builtin: is \"obj\" builtin? no → callee nil. Call trailer i=1 → nil → reject ✓ loud.\n\nNinth: **`sum(genexp, start)`**: args count = 2 → syntax error \"Generator expression must be parenthesized\" ✓ matches CPython.\n\nTenth: **comprehension with nested comprehension in first iterable**: `[x for x in [y for y in z]]` — first iterable is lowered with ctx (outer). scope collect: walk comprehension node → nested comprehension in first iterable is… wait: own comp's `collect`: skip = first-iterable id. walk children: comp_for → walks children including first iterable's or_test — skipped by id? skip checks `(not= skip (:id n))` at walk entry — walk of or_test node is blocked, so the nested comprehension inside it is not registered as a nested scope of the outer comp scope! Hmm wait — the nested comprehension `[y for y in z]` is inside the first iterable, which belongs to the enclosing scope. analyze-scope for the enclosing scope (module/function): its collect walks statements; when it reaches the comprehension node (comprehension? true, not own) → registers as nested, and only walks its first iterable → nested comp `[y for y in z]` inside first iterable → comprehension? → registers as nested of enclosing ✓. And analyze-scope recurses into both comp scopes. OK good — scope nesting is handled: first iterable is walked in enclosing scope's collect.\n\nBut in own comp's collect: skip only prevents first iterable from being double-walked in comp scope. ✓.\n\nAnd lower-comprehension: `fst` is `(lower ctx ...)` with ctx = enclosing ctx lowered ✓, nested comprehension within gets its own scope from enclosing perspective ✓.\n\nEleventh: **comprehension `:locals`** — targets as cells; `%x` symbol is Python name; gen names are unique per node id. Inner comprehension shadowing same name `x`: `[x for x in [x for x in y]]` — both scopes have local \"x\"; inner lam binds x cell; outer lam binds x cell; nested lam shadow at UAST level via lexical binding ✓.\n\nTwelfth: **for loop targets use `assign-target`** — tuple targets in for ✓.\n\nThirteenth: **py/for-each on a list that appends during iteration**: py/iter-at reads live cell each index → sees appends like Python list iteration ✓. But comprehension over a list mutating in emit? Edge.\n\nFourteenth: **slice-positions with float bounds** → py/slice-int raises TypeError ✓ Python: TypeError \"slice indices must be integers or None or have an __index__ method\" ✓ message close enough.\n\nFifteenth: **string slice with non-int step etc** ✓.\n\nSixteenth: **str index with bool?** py/index requires numeric and not float — `l[True]` → py/int? true → num 1 ✓ Python: True as index = 1 ✓.\n\nSeventeenth: **getitem slice on dict/set/range** → py/getitem: dict path doesn't check slice? — for dict t, `(py/key k)` on a slice map → content-type nil → returns slice map as key → lookup miss → KeyError with slice as value. Python: TypeError \"unhashable type: 'slice'\". Deviation P3. Wait actually — py/key on slice map {:py/type :slice ...}: numeric? no; (= (get k :py/type) :tuple) no; content-type: py/cell? no → (get (cell/get x)...) — wait, py/content-type does `(if (py/cell? x) (get (cell/get x) :py/type) nil)` → nil → returns k → uses slice map as dict key → KeyError. Python raises TypeError unhashable slice. Minor P3. Also `d[1:2]` on dict — Python TypeError. Here KeyError. Loud but different. P3.\n\nAlso **tuple getitem with slice** handled ✓; **str with tuple key** — `'ab'[(1,2)]`? py/str? path: py/index k n → py/index checks numeric → tuple not numeric → TypeError ✓.\n\nEighteenth: **negative int step in set-slice: `l[::2] = x`** — lowering statically rejects extended slice assignment ✓ (via slice-parts step check in assign-target). py/set-slice runtime guard NotImplementedError. But note: lowering checks `[_ _ step] (some #(slice-parts ctx %) ...)` — some on subscript_ rules; multi-subscript `l[1:2, 3] = x` → subscript-key rejects multi-dim ✓.\n\nNineteenth: **py/iadd with str b**: `l += \"ab\"` → to-vector → chars ✓ matches Python.\n\nTwentieth: **py/imul negative**: `l *= -1` → repeat with k=-1 → empty ✓ Python.\n\nTwenty-first: **aliasing `a += b` where a is global list** ✓.\n\nTwenty-second: **`L *= 2` alias**: py/imul mutates same cell ✓ Python list.__imul__ is in-place ✓. Test asserts M sees [1,2,1,2] ✓.\n\nTwenty-third: **tuple `t += (1,)`** → py/add new tuple, rebinds ✓ Python.\n\nTwenty-fourth: **`py/eq` str vs tuple etc** ✓. **dict ==** → identity — known deviation (ruled as in-scope in Phase A; still in scope? mappability ruling lists \"dict `==` identity\" as spike deviation scoped. Still open — should we re-note as P3? It was already ruled scoped. Skip or note briefly.)\n\nTwenty-fifth: **`py/truthy` on range** → defaults true (non-empty range object is truthy ✓; range(0) also truthy in Python ✓ — py/obj-truthy handles only cell types; range is non-cell → `(if (py/numeric? x) ... true)` → true ✓.\n\nWait — py/truthy on slice/function/class → true ✓.\n\nTwenty-sixth: **float truthiness 0.0** → py/zero? ✓.\n\nTwenty-seventh: **py/not** ✓.\n\nTwenty-eighth: **py/global-or for builtins**: module's dict wins ✓ (r2 P1 fix preserved).\n\nTwenty-ninth: **read-global \"globals\"**: special case → py/global-or %globals {:py/str \"globals\"} %globals-fn. So `globals()` returns module dict; `global globals; globals = 5` then read → module wins ✓. Matches Python? In Python, `globals` is a builtin function; module can rebind ✓.\n\nBut note: builtin-names map has no \"globals\" — handled by separate branch ✓. What about `globals` used as value: `f = globals; f()['k']` → returns %globals-fn ✓ works.\n\nThirtieth: **py/run-module's gf spec {:params []}** — globals() with args → TypeError ✓.\n\nThirty-first: **exception class**: py.b/NotImplementedError, OverflowError added ✓ base chain correct: NotImplementedError→RuntimeError→Exception ✓; OverflowError→ArithmeticError ✓.\n\nWait — is ValueError missing? Present. StopIteration? Not needed (no generators). BaseException chain ✓.\n\nBut — ValueError used for slice/unpack ✓ present. KeyError ✓.\n\nThirty-second: **`raise` non-BaseException instance accepted** (py/as-exception: any cell) — P3.\n\nThirty-third: **except clause matching uses py/isinstance with exc's class** — also for `except SomeNonClass:` where cls-node evaluates to non-class (e.g. `except None:`) → py/subclass? c None-target: `= c target` false; walks bases to :py/None → false → never matches → re-raise. Python: `except None:` → TypeError \"catching classes that do not inherit from BaseException is not allowed\". Silent divergence P3.\n\nThirty-fourth: **handler stack depth frame** — py/frame's depth arithmetic verified ✓. One concern: py/try's handler frame pushed with saved — depth = saved+1. Nested py/try-finally frames get successive depths. unwind-to compares `(<= (get hs 3) d)`. All consistent.\n\nBut wait — **py/raise for :finally frame runs thunk then re-raises with `(py/raise e)`. If thunk completed but modified handlers (pushed a frame, e.g. thunk itself contains a completed try)** — net same. If thunk leaves a frame pushed (impossible — py/try restores). ✓.\n\n**If thunk runs an escape that does unwind-to below current frame?** Return inside finally during exception propagation: escape's saved depth d_ret < current stack; unwind-to pops frames above d_ret — including frames below the currently-raising frame? The raise has already popped the finally frame before running the thunk; stack during thunk execution = rest. Return escape captured at function entry has saved = stack at function call time, which is below rest. unwind-to(d) pops remaining frames above d, running their finally thunks — but those frames are between function entry and this finally… wait, should they run? Scenario:\n\n```python\ndef f():\n    try:            # frame A (finally)\n        try:        # frame B (finally)\n            raise E\n        finally:    # B's thunk\n            return 1    # return inside finally B during raise\n    finally:        # A's thunk\n        log('A')\n```\nPython: raise E → unwinds: B's finally runs → return inside → replaces exception → A's finally must still run (return propagates through A) → f returns 1, logs A.\n\nTrace here: py/raise pops B frame, runs B thunk(e). Thunk does return → escape (captured at f entry, saved = stack at time of call = base, depth d0). unwind-to(d0): current stack = [A, ...base] (B was popped). Pops A → runs A's thunk with None ✓ → reaches depth ≤ d0 → stops. Then sets handlers := saved, resumes with value 1 ✓. A's finally runs with None — Python runs A's finally during return propagation, no exception in flight (return replaced it) ✓ correct.\n\nBut subtle: A's thunk runs, and inside A's thunk the original raise's `(py/raise e)` continuation never resumes — the escape jumped away. ✓ no double execution.\n\nAnother subtle: **B's thunk running during raise path, doing a normal escape (break) out of loop** — same mechanics ✓.\n\nAnd **escape from finally during normal completion**: py/try-finally's normal path restores handlers before running fin ✓ so fin's return just returns.\n\nThirty-fifth: **py/call-ec's flag cell and re-entry**: on re-entry returns `r` — the value the continuation delivered?? Looking: `((fn [r] (if first (do ... (f escape)) r)) (%capture))` — on re-entry pass, `r` = escape value delivered — py/call-ec returns it as its value ✓ (return value flows).\n\nThirty-sixth: **cost**: every escape invokes unwind-to = 1 cell/get + compare when clean ✓ cheap. py/try allocates flag cell per entry — noted.\n\nThirty-seventh: **determinism — host map iteration**: prelude uses only vectors ✓. lower.cljc compile-time: `(set (keys lower/unsupported-rules))` in test — set of strings, membership ✓. scope: vals iteration into set — order irrelevant ✓. render: iterates snapshots (vectors) ✓. `builtin-names` map is get/contains only ✓. No gensym; gen uses node id ✓ deterministic. `contains-rule?` walks packet's vector ✓.\n\nBut — wait, one: lower.cljc's `display` etc use `(filter p/rule? parts)` — parts = kids vector ✓.\n\n`(partition 2 (filter p/rule? parts))` for dict display — vector ✓.\n\nThirty-eighth: **`(u/lit {:py/str name})`** — literal host map in datoms — encoder must support map literals (spike precedent ✓).\n\nThirty-ninth: **`(u/lit (cond-> {:params params} ...))` in function-value spec** — literal map with vector of strings ✓.\n\nFortieth: **py/pad with defaults count using data/count** — `(py/pad [] (data/count (get spec :kwonly)) 0 [])` — data/count nil → 0 ✓ verified.\n\nForty-first: **py/bind-args when given < n and no star**: pad fills :py/missing; fill-defaults raises if no default ✓. Given > n with star → subvec args n ✓. Given < n with star → `(if (< n given) (data/subvec args n) [])` → empty tuple ✓.\n\nForty-second: **data/subvec** — check exists in data module (host-names includes data/subvec ✓).\n\nForty-third: **kw-extend with non-str keys → TypeError ✓; kw-extend on instance? content-type :instance → not :dict → TypeError \"argument after ** must be a mapping\" ✓.** But Python allows mappings via keys() — subset ✓.\n\nForty-fourth: **duplicate keys in py/kw-pairs across two ** splices**: `f(**{'a':1}, **{'a':2})` → bind-keyword's dict path checks dict-has? → \"multiple values\" TypeError ✓ Python: \"got multiple values for keyword argument 'a'\" ✓.\n\nForty-fifth: **kwargs order**: pairs are in order ✓.\n\nForty-sixth: **call f(*a, *b)** — positional extends twice ✓.\n\nForty-seventh: **`(p/token? (first ak) \"*\")` in argument rule**: argument: `test comp_for? | test '=' test | '**' test | '*' test`. For `k=2`: ak = [test '=' test] count 3 ✓; but for `k = 2` with spaces — packet's children are tokens regardless ✓. For plain positional: ak = [test] count 1 → falls to :else ✓. For genexp argument: scope/comprehension? ✓ checked first.\n\nHmm wait — `(and (= 3 (count ak)) (p/token? (second ak) \"=\"))` — what about `*` check order: cond checks comprehension, then \"*\", then \"**\", then \"=\". For `**d`: first token is \"**\" ✓. But note: `(p/token? (first ak) \"*\")` — for `**d` first token is \"**\" not \"*\" — token? compares text ✓ distinguished.\n\nForty-eighth: **call-parts `seen` set only tracks static keyword names; `f(a=1, **{'a':2})` → runtime dict-has? check ✓.**\n\nForty-ninth: **positional after `*args` unpack**: `f(*a, 1)` → positional after extend — allowed in Python ✓ here: \"*\" arm recurses without kw check on positional; :else arm checks `(when kw ...)` — after *a, kw is nil → OK ✓. Python allows `f(*a, 1)`? Yes since 3.5 ✓.\n\nFiftieth: **`f(k=1, *a)`** → \"*\" arm: `(when kw (syntax! \"iterable argument unpacking follows keyword argument unpacking\"))` — wait, kw is set from k=1 → syntax error with message \"iterable argument unpacking follows keyword argument unpacking\". CPython's message for `f(k=1, *a)`: \"positional argument follows keyword argument\"? Let me think: CPython: `f(1, k=2, *(3,))` → SyntaxError: positional argument follows keyword argument. And `f(k=1, **{}, *a)`… for iterable unpack after keyword, CPython says \"iterable argument unpacking follows keyword argument unpacking\" only when ** precedes *. Actually CPython's ast.c: positional after keyword → \"positional argument follows keyword argument\"; `f(k=1, *a)`: * is iterable unpack (not keyword unpack) so CPython's message: \"iterable argument unpacking follows keyword argument unpacking\"? Hmm, CPython's check: in ast.c's ast_for_call: if follows keyword arg (not **), for positional test: \"positional argument follows keyword argument\"; for * after **: \"iterable argument unpacking follows keyword argument unpacking\". So `f(k=1, *a)` → CPython: \"positional argument follows keyword argument\"? Let me recall exactly… Python 3.8+: `f(k=1, *a)` → SyntaxError: iterable argument unpacking follows keyword argument unpacking? My memory conflicts. Minor message nit — not worth flagging beyond P3. Both are SyntaxError ✓ same class of failure.\n\nNow — **`**` before positional in py/bind-args evaluation order**: golden shows pos built first then kwargs — but evaluation order at runtime: nested py/conj/py/extend applications evaluate operands in order… UAST application's operand evaluation order — presumably left-to-right. `f(*a, k=g())` — a extends then g() called ✓.\n\nFifty-first: **`(u/lit nm)` keyword names in call-parts** — nm is host string; py/bind-keyword compares with `(get pair 0)` = string; py/index-of on spec params (strings) ✓.\n\nFifty-second: **function name `fname`**: `def f` → assign-name f ✓; recursion works via name resolution ✓.\n\nFifty-third: **`__init__` with star args**: py/instantiate → py/call-kw init (into [inst] args) kwargs ✓.\n\nFifty-fourth: **class body with comprehension using class attr in non-first iterable**: resolves via skip ✓ Python semantics.\n\nFifty-fifth: scope.cljc's `collect` — for comprehension scope `own`: body-nodes = [scope-node]; walk skips first iterable; comp_for arm binds target ✓. But — walk for `argument` (genexp) node — comp-clauses handles. In collect, \"comp_for\" arm binds exprlist targets ✓. `if` clauses in comp — walked via default ✓ no binding.\n\nFifty-sixth: **scope: `for_stmt` arm binds exprlist targets — but `for` with single name target: exprlist with one name ✓ target-names handles.\n\nHmm wait — grammar's for_stmt: `'for' exprlist 'in' testlist ':' block` — lower-for gets `(first (rules ctx n \"testlist\"))` — testlist can be `a, b` → display → tuple ✓.\n\nFifty-seventh: **with_item target binding in scope ✓ (\"with_item\" arm).**\n\nFifty-eighth: **except_clause binds as-name ✓; but scope treats as-name as regular local — Python also has as local ✓.**\n\nFifty-ninth: **comprehension inside class body: first iterable in class scope → resolve :class-attr → py/class-ns-get with (u/v (:class ctx))** — in lower-comprehension, `fst` is lowered with `ctx` — ctx of class body has :class set ✓. Test comprehension-scope covers `vals = [i * 2 for i in range(n)]` in class → range(n) reads n via class-ns-get ✓ works.\n\nBut wait — subtle Python rule: first iterable of comprehension in class body can see class attrs ✓ correct.\n\nSixtieth: **lambda in class body defaults reading class attr** — resolve at def time… fine.\n\nNow, remaining checks:\n- e2e_test.clj changes (host-registry, run-python) — quick check.\n- prelude_parity_test.cljc and lower_portable_test.cljc — verify Node parity cases added, reader conditionals with :cljd first.\n- New reader conditionals: `code-unit` in render.cljc has :cljd first ✓ (line 47-50: cljd, clj, cljs ✓). integral? and float-repr NaN: `#?(:cljd ... :clj ... :cljs ...)` ✓ cljd first. render.cljc is new file? No — changed. New conditionals are code-unit (and integral?, negative-zero? may be new). All have :cljd first ✓.\n- Check that `(.charAt ^String s (int i))` on JVM returns char → int ✓; CLJD's `.codeUnitAt` ✓; cljs's `.charCodeAt` ✓.\n\n- **hex2 uses quot/rem on int ✓.**\n\n- **string-repr escape for c=127**: Python escapes \\x7f ✓ (non-printable). Correct.\n\n- Non-ASCII printable passes through ✓ Python 3 repr keeps printable Unicode ✓.\n\n- **float-repr `(< -1e16 x 1e16)`** — cljs: `(< a b c)` works ✓. CLJD: `(< ...)` on num ✓.\n\n- `(= x ##Inf)` — cljs ##Inf ✓; CLJD supports ##Inf literals ✓; JVM ✓.\n\n- CLJS `(str x)` for 0.1 → \"0.1\" ✓; JS shortest round-trip = Python's repr algorithm (both shortest round-trip) — actually JS Number→string is shortest round-trip since ES6? JS uses shortest representation that round-trips (Ryu-like: not exactly — JS spec's Number::toString produces shortest string that round-trips ✓ mostly). Divergence: exponent format (1e21 vs Python's '1e+21'), noted. 0.1+0.2 → \"0.30000000000000004\" ✓ both.\n\n- JVM `(str 0.1)` → \"0.1\" ✓ (Clojure prints doubles shortest since 1.7? Uses Double/toString → \"0.1\" ✓; Double/toString not always shortest round-trip (pre-JDK19 is shortest that uniquely distinguishes — mostly matches). Documented boundary 1e16/1e-4. Fine — already noted as P3 on r2 gate (float repr divergence documented).\n\n- **py/len on range iterates** — noted as P3 cost.\n- **py/contains on range materializes** — noted as P3 cost.\n- **py/range-at with huge range in for loop** — for i in range(10**15) → loops one at a time (Python also loops but O(1) memory; here O(1) memory too ✓ since iter-at is lazy). Only `in`/len materialize.\n\n- **py/iterable on str for for-loops** — decodes to tuple of chars once ✓ O(n) memory, fine.\n\n- **py/for-each for comprehension on str**: `[c for c in 'ab']` — comp's first iterable `fst` = py/iterable? lower-comprehension's body: first clause is `(py/for-each (u/v fst) ...)` — py/for-each applies py/iterable internally ✓ (`py/for-each (fn [x f] (py/for-each-at (py/iterable x) f 0))`) ✓.\n\nWait — but `fst` was lowered as raw iterable value; lower-comprehension doesn't wrap in py/iterable but py/for-each does ✓. Test `list(c for c in 'ab')` → chars ✓ confirmed.\n\n- **for-each with multiple clauses**: subsequent iterables `(lower cctx b)` → py/for-each handles str ✓.\n\n- **`assign-target cctx a (u/v x)`** — tuple targets inside comprehension `[(a,b) for a,b in pairs]` ✓.\n\nNow — **u/let1 for `fst` before locals cell allocation**: first iterable evaluated in enclosing env; cell allocation after — but comprehension's locals include target names; what if first iterable references same name (e.g. `[x for x in x]` where x is outer local)? fst lowered with ctx → outer x ✓; cell allocated after, shadowing ✓ Python: `[x for x in x]` works, iterating outer x ✓.\n\nNow — **`(apply u/app (u/lam (map symbol (:locals s)) result) cells)`** — symbols are Python names; can clash with generated % names? Python names don't start with % ✓. Can comprehension's locals shadow outer let-bound gensyms? Python names ≠ % names ✓.\n\nBut — **comprehension inside method: locals bound as lam params shadow outer function locals with same Python name** ✓ desired.\n\nSixty-first: **u/lam with duplicate param names?** If :locals s contains duplicates — assigned dedupes via bind! (only conj if not some) ✓ params… comprehension scope :locals = assigned only (kind :comprehension → `(into params others)` — comprehension params = [] ✓).\n\nHmm wait — scope record :locals for comprehension: `(case kind :module [] :class (vec others) (into params others))` — comprehension → into [] others ✓.\n\nSixty-second: **function scope :locals = params + others; function-value's all-params = (:params s)** — from params-of — all named params in written order ✓ and cells allocated in order matching bind-args vector ✓. But — duplicate params: caught by syntax check ✓. And **kwonly params in :locals?** others excludes params; params includes kwonly names ✓; cells allocated for all params in order; bind-args vector layout is positional, star, kwonly, kwstar ✓ matches written order (params-of order = written order, includes star name and kwstar name in position) ✓.\n\nWait, verify: `def f(a, *args, c, **kw)` — params-of returns [\"a\" \"args\" \"c\" \"kw\"] ✓. bind-args: slots [a], with-star [a tuple], with-ko [a tuple c], +kw → [a tuple c kw-dict] ✓ same order.\n\n`def f(a, *, c)` — bare star: params-of: '*' token is not tfpdef → not collected… typedargslist children: [tfpdef(a) ',' '*' ',' tfpdef(c)]? For bare '*', does grammar alternative `| '*' tfpdef? (...)` include '*'? params-of filters only tfpdef/vfpdef rules → [\"a\" \"c\"] ✓. bind-args: star? false → [a] + ko [c] → [a c] ✓.\n\n`def f(*, c)` → params [\"c\"], spec params [] kwonly [c] ✓ bind → [c] ✓.\n\nSixty-third: **defaults evaluation order**: `(build-vector (map lower defaults))` at def time, left-to-right ✓ Python.\n\nDefault expressions referencing preceding params — Python evaluates defaults in enclosing scope at def time ✓ here lowered with ctx (enclosing) ✓ correct (params are not in scope for defaults).\n\nSixty-fourth: **kwonly defaults at def time** ✓ (same map, ctx enclosing).\n\nSixty-fifth: **`gen` names for args vector: `(gen \"args\" scope-node)`** — %argsN symbol; u/lam binds it; `(app* 'py/arg (u/v args) i)` inside ✓.\n\nNow, remaining checks: e2e_test.clj changes, portable tests, and whether py/no-handler is handled in all four VMs (pre-existing from spike ✓). Let me quickly look at prelude_parity_test.cljc additions and e2e_test.clj diff areas. Also verify `data/str-index-of` exists for substring ✓ (included in host-names; data module is master and unchanged? git status shows only listed files changed; data.cljc unchanged — so data/str-index-of and str->code-points must already exist on master. Let me verify exports exist.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Let me verify the data module exports the prelude's host-names, and check the"}]}}
