{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_end","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Tests look good. Notes:\n\n- `x = a @ b` rejection: \"operator @\" — grammar alternative 4 contains '@', lower-expr's binary arm rejects ✓. `x @= b` \"augmented @=\" ✓.\n- Rule classification: `(set (seq Python3Parser/ruleNames))` = union of handled/consumed/unsupported, pairwise disjoint ✓.\n- Interesting rejection: `f(i for i in y)` — \"argument\" rule genexp → unsupported ✓ (f is not a known consuming builtin… wait, `f` is unresolvable → builtin? false → callee nil → not in consuming-builtins → unsupported ✓).\n- Static errors cover duplicate keywords, positional after keyword, bare *, multiple stars, etc.\n\nThings not covered by tests: `except (A, B):` — confirmed no coverage anywhere (grep found no \"except (\" within tests). Let me verify whether this bug actually exists: lower-try-except's `(app* 'py/isinstance (u/v exc) (lower cls-node))`. cls-node = `(first (rules ctx clause \"test\"))` — for `except (A, B):`, the test rule wraps an atom parenthesized testlist_comp containing commas → lower → display → py/tuple. py/isinstance(o, tuple) → py/subclass?(class, tuple-map) → never equal → false → falls through → re-raises. In Python it would catch either. So `except (A, B):` silently fails to catch. This is a P1/P2 issue — silent misbehavior on a core try-statement form listed in the language reference. try/except is in scope (Phase B had except; C1 added finally). The brief says \"rejected constructs fail loudly\" — but this isn't rejected, it's silently wrong. Since try/except with tuple is a core Python construct supported from Phase B (pre-existing issue?) — wait, was it in the Phase B spike? The except clause was in the spike. Did the spike claim to support `except (A,B)`? Probably it had the same py/isinstance issue. Pre-existing issue, but C1's brief item 1 explicitly covers try/except/finally semantics (\"handler stack integrity across nested finally/try/loops; tests