{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All reader conditionals have `:cljd` first ✓. No gensym/rand ✓. scope.cljc uses volatile! — compile-time only, single-threaded, deterministic accumulation order (vector). OK.\n\nNext, check scope_test.clj for the comprehensions scope tests (especially the class-body case and the first-iterable lambda). And I want to verify a few remaining semantic risks:\n\n1. `py/is` on maps/tuples uses host `=` — the deviation of \"tuple is tuple\" (P3).\n2. `except (A, B)` never matches — a P1/P2 finding.\n3. `py/contains` on a range gets materialized — a P3 cost.\n4. break/continue inside finally is accepted (Python: SyntaxError) — P3.\n5. `py/as-exception` accepts any cell (e.g. `raise [1]`) — P3.\n6. In the except clause, if cls is a non-class → never matches (Python raises TypeError) — P3 (related to 2).\n7. The `as` name in except is not deleted after the block — P3.\n8. Dunder lookup goes through instance attributes (py/getattr checks its own attrs first); Python looks up special methods on the type — `mgr.__exit__ = x` on the instance gets used here. P3.\n9. Integer overflow beyond 2^53 diverges across hosts (JVM throws ArithmeticException via Clojure's *; JS goes silently inaccurate; Dart?) — P2? Let me think a bit more.\n\nAbout #9: On JVM, Clojure's `*` on longs: throws ArithmeticException on overflow? Actually, Clojure's `*` on longs does… Clojure 1.3+ semantics: +,-,* on longs throw ArithmeticException \"integer overflow\" on overflow, unless you use unchecked or auto-promoting +' *'. Wait, no — is that right? Clojure: does `(* 9223372036854775807 2)` throw ArithmeticException? Hmm, actually I thought Clojure's `*` auto-promotes to BigInt! Let me remember precisely: Clojure 1.3 introduced \"contagion\" — `+`, `*` promote longs to BigInt on overflow. `(+ 9223372036854775807 1)` → 9223372036854775808 (BigInt), doesn't throw. Yes — Clojure's + and * auto-promote to BigInt. It's `+'` that's… no wait, it's the opposite: `+` throws and `+'` promotes? No: Clojure docs: \"+'  like + but auto-promotes on overflow\"; \"+ throws ArithmeticException on overflow\"? Let me remember for real: (long overflow) — `(inc Long/MAX_VALUE)` → ArithmeticException? No! `inc` throws on overflow. But `+`: `(+ Long/MAX_VALUE 1)` → also ArithmeticException? I think bare + throws \"integer overflow\" and +' promotes. From Clojure docs: \"Clojure provides a set of arithmetic operators that throw on overflow (+, -, *) and auto-promoting variants (+', -', *').\" Yes, that's right: + throws, +' promotes to BigInt.\n\nSo on JVM: `2 ** 100` → does py/ipow use `*`? ipow: `(* h h)` — bare `*` → ArithmeticException on long overflow → host error, not loud. The report acknowledges: \"JVM long overflow in `**` is still a host error.\" On JS: `2**100` → py/ipow uses `*` on doubles → 1.2676506002282294e30 exact (power of 2, double can represent it) → prints \"1.2676506002282294e+30\"? Rendering: `(number? v) (str v)` — JS str of 1.267e30 → \"1.2676506002282294e+30\". On Dart: int overflow wraps? CLJD's host integer: `*` on Dart ints → wraps at 64 bits? Dart VM ints are 64-bit signed and wrap on overflow (no throw). 2**100 → wraps to some 64-bit value → prints a completely different number.\n\nSo `print(2 ** 100)`: JVM → host ArithmeticException (task failure), Node → \"1.2676506002282294e+30\", Dart → some wrapped garbage value. Three different behaviors across VMs/hosts on the same guest program. This violates determinism (\"same behavior on all four VMs\"). Reachable from fully supported syntax (int literal ≤ 2^53, `**` is supported). Even `10 ** 16` = 10000000000000000 > 2^53 ≈ 9.007e15! `print(10 ** 16)`: JVM: 10^16 fits in long → 10000000000000000 ✓. Node: double is exact ✓ prints 10000000000000000 ✓. Dart ✓. At 10^16 all fine. `10 ** 17`: JVM long OK. Node: 1e17 exact as double? 10^17 = 100000000000000000 — double has 53-bit mantissa; 1e17 is exactly representable (10^17 = 2^17 * 5^17; 5^17 = 762939453125 < 2^53 ✓ so exact). Prints as \"100000000000000000\"? JS str(1e17) → \"100000000000000000\" ✓. Up to about 1e21 → str becomes \"1e+21\" — different from JVM's \"1000000000000000000000\". So `print(10 ** 21)` → JVM \"1000000000000000000000\" vs Node \"1e+21\" — divergent printed output, both \"valid.\" And 2**63: JVM ArithmeticException; Node exact \"9223372036854775808\"; Dart wraps negative.\n\nSo yes: cross-host divergence on supported syntax. The ns docstring says the algorithms are \"valid within 2^53\" — documented limitation — but the failure mode beyond 2^53 isn't uniform (not even consistently loud): JVM throws a host error (does it surface as an in-test [:thrown ...], or in prod as task failure?), JS silently prints inaccurate/exponential notation, Dart silently wraps. The gate brief explicitly asks about \"the integer algorithm's 2^53 bound and its cost.\"\n\nSeverity: The determinism invariant is non-negotiable in this project. But the spike phase already had this issue with `*` (py/arith * is preexisting — `(py/arith * a b)` uses host `*`). So `10**21` divergence via bare multiplication is preexisting. C1 adds `**` and `<<` which make it much more reachable, and adds py/ipow/py/lshift/py/bit-op which have the same unguarded problem. Since the brief says big ints are deferred to a later slice, a uniform guard (raise OverflowError when result is outside ±2^53) is the natural fix, and the precedent is py/floor. I'll rate this P2: silent cross-host divergence on accepted programs; fix = magnitude guard in py/arith (int path), py/ipow, py/lshift, py/bit-op — or at minimum in ipow/lshift. Hmm — but is this \"accepted\"? The 2^53 bound is documented as a validity limit. Guest programs that exceed it produce divergent host behavior — that violates determinism regardless of documentation. I'll rate P2.\n\nWait — let me actually re-verify Dart's behavior on int overflow: CLJD compiles Clojure `*` to Dart `*`. Dart int: 64-bit, wraps on overflow? Dart language spec: on Dart VM, int is 64-bit two's complement, arithmetic wraps on overflow? Actually, Dart's int overflow: \"On the VM, int values are 64-bit signed integers and arithmetic wraps on overflow\" — hmm, I think Dart VM ints saturate? No — Dart VM wraps (like C). dart2js uses doubles. CLJD targets the Dart VM → 64-bit wrap. Yes.\n\nAnd JVM Clojure `*`: throws ArithmeticException \"integer overflow\" — I'm fairly sure bare +,-,* throw on long overflow (Clojure 1.3+). And what does the host error do inside a VM run — the task raises, in tests the ex-message shows up as [:thrown ...]. In production: task failure — loud but not guest-level, and host-specific.\n\nActually — wait, one more consideration: does py/ipow overflow even reach `*` with longs? ipow(2, 100): h=ipow(2,50) = 2^50 long OK; hh = (* h h) = 2^100 → long overflow → ArithmeticException on JVM ✓.\n\nAnd py/bit-op for values ≤ 2^53 stays ≤ 2^53 ✓ (result bounded by operands). lshift: `(* a (ipow 2 n))` — n up to… `1 << 62` → ipow is 2^62 exact on long; * → 2^62 OK; `1 << 63` → JVM: (* 1 2^63)? ipow 2 63 → 9223372036854775808 overflows long → ArithmeticException on JVM; Node: 9.22e18 exact double → prints 9223372036854775808; Dart: wraps to -9223372036854775808. Divergence ✓ same problem.\n\nOK. Next — also check `py/add` on ints: host `+` — same preexisting issue (spike). Not introduced by C1, but C1's `**`/`<<` amplify. I'll write P2 targeting the new operations (ipow, lshift) with a note that py/arith shares the flaw.\n\nHmm, but wait — was this already flagged in a previous gate? The r2 gate findings didn't cover it (spike had * and +; r2's 5 P3s didn't include overflow). The mappability ruling says \"Integer overflow beyond 64 bits on JVM → scope: bignum path in numeric encoding\" — acknowledged as scope. But the ruling treats it as a JVM-only issue (\"beyond 64 bits\"); the cross-host divergence (JS double inaccuracy at 2^53, Dart wrap at 2^63, JVM throw at 2^63) within [2^53, 2^63) is broader. Given documented scope deferral, I'll rate P2 (determinism) — borderline P2/P3. The gate brief explicitly asks: \"the integer algorithm's 2^53 bound and its cost.\" My answer: bound documented but not enforced; divergence above it. Fix is cheap (guard in ipow/lshift/arith int path, py/floor precedent raises OverflowError), and since determinism is a non-negotiable invariant, P2 is justified. Actually — let me reconsider severity: to exceed 2^53 the guest program needs to compute a big value; all e2e tests stay within bound. The failure isn't silent in most cases on JVM (throws). On JS it's silently wrong (inaccurate or exponential notation). Given the \"no silent divergence\" invariant, I'll keep P2 but note scope deferral context.\n\n3'. Also check `py/repeat` cost: `'ab' * 10**9` → repeat-str recurses n times, building O(n) string — host memory explosion. Python would also make a 2GB string (MemoryError). Roughly equivalent. `range-len` on `range(10**15)`: `len(range(10**15))` in Python is O(1); here O(n) → effectively hangs (10^15 iterations of continuation). Reachable: `print(len(range(1000000000000)))` — wait, literals are limited to 2^53 ≈ 9e15, and 1e12 is allowed. len(range(1e12)) → 1e12 loop steps → hours. Python is instant. This is a cost/DoS divergence — the gate brief asks about cost. P3 with fix suggestion: closed-form range length: max(0, ceil((stop-start)/step)) in integer arithmetic.\n\nSimilarly py/contains on a range: `x in range(1e12)` materializes → OOM/hang. P3 same fix family: closed-form membership test for ranges (arithmetic check).\n\npy/floor-descend: O(53) ✓ fine. py/divmod-pos: O(log(a/b)) ✓ fine. py/bit-op: O(bits) ~54 levels, each with divmod O(log) ✓ fine. py/int-floordiv used in loops (py/ipow recursion) fine.\n\n`py/range-at` per iteration O(1) ✓ for loops over big ranges are linear in iteration count — matches Python ✓.\n\npy/to-vector on str decodes whole thing ✓ fine.\n\npy/collect on list O(n) ✓.\n\nNext — check scope_test additions. And then verify the \"0755\" claim: grammar lexer — the test asserts the parser produces a syntax-error packet for \"x = 0755\" ✓ pinned by test. And \"00\" is OK ✓ matches Python.\n\nWait — verify Python: `x = 00` valid? Python 3: `00` → 0 ✓ valid (multiple zeros allowed). And `0_0` → valid = 0. Here: t=\"00\" no [1-9] → parse-radix → 0 ✓. `01` → error ✓ Python: SyntaxError ✓.\n\nHmm, edge case: what about \"0\" itself? starts-with \"0\", re-find [1-9] nil → radix → 0 ✓.\n\nFloat \"0.0\"? Handled by the float branch before the leading-zero check ✓ (Python: 0.0 valid ✓). But \"00.5\"? Python: `00.5` → valid = 0.5! Here: t=\"00.5\" → re-find [.eE] → float branch → 0.5 ✓ good.\n\n\"0e0\"? Float branch ✓ Python valid ✓.\n\nEdge case: \"0x\" prefix check before leading zero ✓. What about \"0_1\"? → t=\"01\" → error. Python: `0_1` — hmm, actually let me think again. Python 3.6+: underscores in numeric literals. Tokenizer: \"0_1\" — is this valid? Rule: leading zeros still forbidden even with underscores: `0_1` → SyntaxError? I recall `1_000` is valid, `0_0` is valid, `0_1`… CPython's error \"leading zeros in decimal integer literals are not permitted\" applies to `01`; for `0_1`, does CPython tokenize NUMBER \"0_1\"? Grammar for decimalinteger: `nonzerodigit digit* | \"0\"+` — with underscores: the actual CPython lexer pattern for decimal integer allows underscores between digits: is `0_1` matched by the pattern? CPython tokenizer decimalinteger: `0_[0_]*0 | [1-9][0-9_]* | 0`. Hmm — `0_1`: doesn't match `0_[0_]*0` (ends in 1), doesn't match `[1-9]...`, doesn't match bare `0` (has _1). But imagnumber/float patterns… `0_1` → tokenized as `0` NAME `_1`? That would be a syntax error in an expression context ✓. Actually CPython: `x = 0_1` → SyntaxError: invalid decimal literal. Yes, error ✓ matches.\n\nWhat does the pinned ANTLR lexer do for \"0_1\"? If it produces NUMBER \"0_1\" → lower guard: t = \"01\" → syntax! ✓. If the lexer rejects → syntax-error packet ✓. Either way loud ✓.\n\nNext, scope_test.clj — let me quickly check the comprehensions tests. Also the e2e_test.clj fixes (escape-restores-handlers-test etc., preexisting). Check scope_test for the placement of the first-iterable lambda and the class-body comprehensions.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Reader conditionals all have `:cljd"}]}}
