{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"str-length is the code point count ✓ Python's len(str) ✓.\n\nOK, so let me trace through the lowering of except_clause one more time, and re-verify the suspicion about `except (A, B)` against the shape of the grammar. except_clause: `'except' (test ('as' NAME)?)? ':'`. In the case of `except (A, B):` — test wraps atom '(' testlist_comp ')' with a comma → display → `(py/tuple [A B])`. lower-try-except: `(app* 'py/isinstance (u/v exc) <tuple node>)`. py/isinstance(o cls): o is an instance → `(py/subclass? (get (cell/get o) :class) cls)`. py/subclass?(c target): c is a class cell; target = tuple map. `(= c :py/None)` is false; `(= c target)` — cell-ref map vs tuple map — false; recurse on base → eventually base is :py/None → false. In other words, it never matches → falls through to next clause or re-raises. Bug confirmed: the exception slips past the tuple handler and propagates (in e2e, an uncaught exception would appear) — but the crucial thing is that the program silently continues to behave differently from Python.\n\nAnd `isinstance(x, (A, B))` → py/b/isinstance → similarly → False, whereas in Python it would be True. Silently wrong boolean. isinstance is a supported builtin (has existed since the spike), and the tuple form is standard Python. Is this existing (spike) or new? py/isinstance has existed since the spike, with the same behavior. C1 didn't touch it except for the spec addition. The gate's scope is the entire diff, but findings from a fresh review are also in scope (\"check Python 3 semantics for the claimed subset\"). try/except is within the claimed subset (\"try/except/else/finally\"). The tuple form of except is part of the try statement semantics in the language reference. Flag as P2 (existing but in scope, silently wrong) — hmm, previous gates have let this slip; C1's brief item 1 says \"try/finally and try/except/else/finally … tests for each exit kind and nesting.\" The tuple except form is neither tested nor rejected.\n\nSeverity calibration: P0 = blocker/crash/corruption; P1 = silently wrong semantics on a claimed-supported feature; P2 = wrong/divergent on an edge, or loud but host-dependent; P3 = minor. `except (A,B)` silently failing to catch = wrong control flow on a common, claimed-supported statement form → P1. `isinstance(x, (A,B))` → silently wrong boolean → P1. Make the tuple class handling a single P1 finding (single root cause, two symptoms).\n\nHmm, but wait — let me confirm one more time: might the lowering of except_clause reject tuples somewhere? cls-node = `(first (rules ctx clause \"test\"))`. No tuple check. And the unsupported list? None. Confirmed.\n\nNo wait, let me reconsider — in Python `except (A, B)` is — is this within the \"claimed subset\"? The report says \"try/finally and try/except/else/finally run the finally block on every exit.\" except is claimed. The language reference for except says: the expression list is evaluated; if a tuple, matches any. So a claimed-supported statement form behaves silently differently → P1. But the mitigating factor: this is existing spike behavior (not introduced by C1). The gate reviews the diff, but a fresh full review is also instructed (\"check Python 3 semantics for the claimed subset against the language reference: finally on every exit … nesting\"). The claimed subset includes try/except semantics. Flag as P1, note that it's existing, and fix is cheap: make py/isinstance iterate tuple cls, or reject tuple except in the lowering with a bounded diagnostic (the loud-fail invariant demands one or the other).\n\nHmm, actually — wait. Let me consider whether to reject or support. Owner's policy: simple, tedious mapping. py/isinstance supporting tuple cls: `(if (= (get cls :py/type) :tuple) (any-element-matches) ...)` — cheap. Either fix works.\n\nNext, the 2^53 overflow divergence — P2.\n\nNext, verify **whether py/raise correctly pops HANDLER frames positioned above finally frames** … raise walks the stack from the top: what if a handler frame (from an inner py/try whose body completed?) has been left on the stack? py/try restores handlers after body completion, so a stale handler frame never remains. After the handler runs (re-entry), handlers = saved. ✓\n\nOne more: **does py/try's flag cell leak across calls?** flag is fresh per call ✓.\n\n**py/call-ec re-entry value**: on re-entry, the wrapper returns `r` — the delivered value ✓.\n\nNow — **py/try's orelse runs with handlers := saved, and else-block raises → propagates to outer handler ✓ correct Python**.\n\n**Handler running: handlers := saved — exceptions inside the handler propagate outward past this try ✓ correct**.\n\n**Bare `raise` inside except (re-raise)**: lower-flow raise_stmt empty tests → `(app* 'py/raise (u/v exc))` with :exc ctx — hctx has :exc exc ✓. Note: Python clears the \"active exception\" after the except block; bare raise inside finally nested within an except? Edge. Also bare raise inside a nested function inside except → :exc nil in function ctx → unsupported \"bare raise outside except\" — hmm, in Python bare raise inside a nested function raises RuntimeError (no active exception), but static rejection is loud ✓ no problem.\n\nBut — **bare `raise` in an except handler re-raises with the original traceback; here raise walks from handlers = saved (the handler's frame is not on the stack)**. An enclosing finally frame above saved? Example:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    try:\n        raise E\n    except E:\n        raise     # bare\nfinally:\n    log()\n```\nBare raise: py/raise e — stack has the outer finally frame → executes with e → continues to handler ✓ correct.\n\nNext — **`raise` inside a finally thunk during raise unwinding** (replacement): tested ✓ (raise-in-finally test).\n\nNext — **with-stmt `as` with a starred target?** `with m as (a, *b):` — assign-target handles ✓.\n\n**With body containing a comprehension** ✓ no problem.\n\nNext verify **`py/with`'s getattr on `mgr` for non-cell (e.g., `with 5:`)** → py/getattr 5 \"__exit__\" → attr-error AttributeError ✓ Python: AttributeError 'int' object has no attribute '__exit__'… Python actually: AttributeError: __exit__ ✓ loud.\n\nNext — **`py/type-of` on exception instance** ✓.\n\nNext — determinism check on new compile-time code: `(set/union ...)` in tests is fine.\n\n**Chunk invariance** tested ✓.\n\nNext — verify the additions to the refusal list: \"x = (i for i in y)\" — atom arm: inner is testlist_comp comprehension → unsupported \"generator expression (phase C2)\" ✓. But — `x = [i for i in y]` is a list comprehension ✓. `(i for i in y)` as a sole argument in a call → handled. `for x in (i for i in y):` → for's testlist → display → single test → ... lower → atom '(' inner comprehension → unsupported ✓ loud.\n\nNext — **test_nocond inside comp_if**: `[:if (first (rules ctx n \"test_nocond\"))]` ✓. lambdef_nocond inside comprehension if: `[x for x in y if (lambda: 1)()]`... fine.\n\n**comp_for exprlist target**: `(first (rules ctx n \"exprlist\"))` ✓.\n\nNext — **comp_for's `or_test` iterable**: grammar comp_for: ['async'] 'for' exprlist 'in' or_test [comp_iter]. In Python `for x in a if b else c` — does or_test exclude conditional? In Python the comprehension iterable is a full `testlist`... Actually in Python `[x for x in (a if b else c)]` requires parens; `[x for x in a if b]` — this `if` is comp_if. The grammar uses or_test ✓ matches CPython's grammar (testlist in old grammar; this g4 uses or_test — matches python3.g4 ✓).\n\nNext — **scope.cljc's first-iterable**: `(first (p/child-rules pk (first (p/child-rules pk comp-node \"comp_for\")) \"or_test\"))` ✓ same node.\n\n**skip in collect**: `(when own (:id (first-iterable pk own)))` — for genexp's `argument` node comp-node ✓ same.\n\nNext — lower-comprehension for genexp consumed by builtin: `(lower-comprehension ctx genexp :list)` where genexp is an `argument` node. rule-kids of argument: [or_test comp_for]? comps: `(filterv #(not (p/rule? % \"comp_for\")) (rule-kids ctx comp-node))` → [or_test] → element = or_test → lower → emit appends or_test's value ✓.\n\nNext — **`(u/lam [x] ...)` comprehension loop variable x = (gen \"x\" a)** where a is the exprlist target node; assign-target cctx a (u/v x) → cell set ✓.\n\n**Multiple for-clauses**: reduce builds nested py/for-each from innermost (reverse) ✓ correct nesting order: first clause outermost ✓.\n\n**if-clause between for-clauses**: `[x for x in a if p for y in b]` — clauses in order; reduce from reversed: emit; :for b wraps emit; :if p wraps that; :for a wraps. So for a { if p { for b { emit } } } ✓ correct.\n\nNext — **`(py/for-each (lower cctx b) ...)`** iterables after the first: py/for-each applies py/iterable ✓ str handling ✓.\n\nNow, remaining checks: **uast.cljc is unchanged** (not in git status) ✓. **stage.cljc unchanged** ✓.\n\n**e2e_test.clj changes**: escape-restores-handlers-test (line 333), function-objects-test, too-many-arguments-test — modified for the new calling convention. Let me quickly check lines 130–260 and look at function-objects/too-many-arguments to see spec-based errors. Actually, the full suite passes (verified by orchestrator). I'll skim just to confirm the TypeError message changes didn't lose coverage of old behavior.\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Let me check the modified e2e tests for the new call convention, and the `escape-restores-handlers"}]}}
