hem first exposed a coverage gap in the slice tests, which I closed.\n18: \n19: **Not done.**\n20: - cljstyle is blocked by permission in this session.\n21: - `bb test:cljd` was not run, per the brief.\n22: \n23: Work is in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-py-c1`, on branch `yang-python-phase-c1`. Nothing is staged or committed.\n24: \n25: ## Supported now\n26: \n27: **1. `finally` and `with`.**\n28: - `try`/`finally` and `try`/`except`/`else`/`finally` run the finally block on every exit: normal completion, `return`, `break`, `continue` and `raise`.\n29: - Nested finally blocks run innermost first.\n30: - A `return` inside `finally` overrides the pending return or exception.\n31: - An exception raised inside `finally` replaces the one already in flight.\n32: - `with a as x, b:` follows the language reference's expansion:\n33:   - `__exit__` is looked up before `__enter__` runs;\n34:   - on an exception, `__exit__(type, value, None)` is called, and a truthy result suppresses the exception;\n35:   - every other exit calls `__exit__(None, None, None)`;\n36:   - the `as` target can be any assignment target.\n37: \n38: **2. Tuples, unpacking and slices.**\n39: - Tuples have value semantics:\n40:   - `==` compares elements (so `(1, 2) == (1.0, 2)`);\n41:   - they are hashable when their elements are, so `d[1, 2]` and `d[(1.0, 2)]` find the same key;\n42:   - lists, dicts and sets used as keys raise TypeError \"unhashable type\".\n43: - Tuple and list targets unpack, nested to any depth, including `x, (y, z) = ...`, `[d] = ...`, `for k, v in d.items():` and `with m as (p, q):`.\n44: - One starred target per assignment is allowed (`first, *rest = ...`).\n45: - Unpacking with the wrong count raises ValueError.\n46: - Slices work on lists, tuples and strings, with start, stop and step, negative indexes, and clamping exactly as `slice.indices` does it.\n47: - Slice assignment works on lists for an absent step or a step of 1. An extended slice target is rejected.\n48: \n49: **3. Operators.**\n50: - `**` on ints, and on floats with an integral exponent. A negative exponent returns a float, and `0 ** -n` raises ZeroDivisionError.\n51: - `%` and `//` use Python's floor semantics for negative operands, on both ints and floats, and raise ZeroDivisionError on a zero divisor.\n52: - `& | ^ ~ << >>` on ints, using unbounded two's complement as Python does. A negative shift count raises ValueError.\n53: - `in` and `not in` work for lists, tuples, ranges, str (substring), dict keys and sets.\n54: - All the new augmented forms work. `+=` and `*=` on a list mutate it in place, so aliases see the change, matching `list.__iadd__`.\n55: - Also added: list, tuple and str concatenation and repetition.\n56: \n57: **4. Comprehensions.**\n58: - List, dict and set comprehensions, with multiple `for` and `if` clauses.\n59: - Each comprehension has its own scope:\n60:   - its targets are cells local to the comprehension;\n61:   - the first iterable is evaluated in the enclosing scope, so a class body can use its own names there;\n62:   - lambdas inside the comprehension share the loop variable's single cell, so `[lambda: i for i in range(3)]` gives 2, 2, 2.\n63: - A generator expression is accepted only as the sole argument of `list`, `tuple`, `set`, `sum`, `any` or `all`, and only when that name resolves to the builtin. There it is lowered as a list, which is not observably different. Any other generator expression is rejected as \"generator expression (phase C2)\".\n64: \n65: **5. Keyword arguments.**\n66: - Call sites accept `name=value`, `*iterable` and `**mapping`.\n67: - Definitions accept keyword-only parameters (after `*name` or a bare `*`), keyword-only defaults, and `**kwargs`.\n68: - All binding failures are Python TypeErrors: too many positional arguments, multiple values for an argument, an unexpected keyword, a missing positional argument, a missing keyword-only argument, and `**` applied to a non-mapping or with non-string keys.\n69: - The static syntax errors are: a repeated keyword, a positional argument after a keyword argument, named arguments missing after a bare `*`, and a non-default argument after a default one.\n70: \n71: **6. The five P3 notes from the r2 gate.**\n72: - **(a) Implicit `object` base and the exception classes the prelude raises: left unchanged, because the current behaviour is CPython's.** CPython uses `builtins.object` for an implicit base, and its interpreter-raised errors are `builtins.TypeError` and friends regardless of what the module binds those names to. A test now pins it: after `TypeError = None; object = 1`, a class still defines and an internal TypeError is still caught by `except Exception`.\n73: - **(b) `global globals; globals()` now works.** `globals` is a per-module function object, passed to the module body as `%globals-fn` and read through the same \"module key wins, else builtin\" path as every other builtin. It can also be used as a value now.\n74: - **(c) An out-of-range `\\U` escape is now a qualified syntax diagnostic** (\"illegal Unicode character in \\U escape\"), not a host exception.\n75: - **(d) `0755`: the r2 note does not hold for the pinned grammar.** Its lexer has no token for that literal, so the parser already emits a syntax-error packet; a test now pins that. I added a matching lowering guard in case a grammar ever passes one through.\n76: - **(e) repr fixes:**\n77:   - UnboundLocalError's message is now CPython 3.11's: \"cannot access local variable 'x' where it is not associated with a value\".\n78:   - repr picks quotes the way Python does: `\"it's\"` gets double quotes.\n79:   - `\\n`, `\\r` and `\\t` are escaped, and other control characters as `\\xNN`.\n80: \n81: ## Still rejected (each with a qualified diagnostic)\n82: \n83: - **For C2:** generator expressions anywhere other than the consuming-builtin position, and `yield`.\n84: - **Imports and statements:** imports, `del`, `assert`, `match`, `async`/`await`, and decorators.\n85: - **Display and argument forms:** `@` and `@=`, `{**d}` in a display, multi-dimensional slicing, extended slice assignment, and multiple inheritance.\n86: - **Literals:** f-strings, bytes, and integers above 2^53 (big ints are a later slice).\n87: - **Annotations:** parameter and return annotations.\n88: - **`**` with a non-integral exponent** (for example `2 ** 0.5`). This is a runtime NotImplementedError, not a static rejection, because the runtime exponent is not known statically and there is no portable `pow`/`exp`.\n89: \n90: ## Rule → Universal AST changes\n91: \n92: The \"every grammar rule classified\" test passes and has been updated. These rules moved out of the unsupported set:\n93: \n94: | Rule | Now | Lowers to |\n95: |---|---|---|\n96: | `with_stmt` | handled | `(py/with mgr (fn [%wv] (assign target %wv) body))`, nested right to left for several items |\n97: | `with_item` | consumed by `with_stmt` | (see `with_stmt`) |\n98: | `star_expr` | handled | splice `py/extend` in displays and in `*` call arguments; `py/unpack-star` in targets; a syntax error anywhere else |\n99: | `sliceop` | consumed by `subscript_` | `(py/slice start stop step)` |\n100: | `comp_for`, `comp_iter`, `comp_if` | consumed by the comprehension | `py/for-each` loops and `if` filters inside the comprehension |\n101: | `test_nocond` | handled | lowered to its single child |\n102: | `lambdef_nocond` | handled | same as `lambdef` |\n103: \n104: Existing rules changed as follows:\n105: \n106: | Rule | Change |\n107: |---|---|\n108: | `try_stmt` | A `finally` clause becomes `(py/try-finally (fn [] <try/except part>) (fn [%fx] <finally>))`. |\n109: | `testlist`, `testlist_star_expr`, `exprlist` | One element is lowered as before. Several elements, a trailing comma or a star produce `(py/tuple <elements>)`. A lone `*a` is a syntax error. |\n110: | `atom` | `()` is an empty tuple; a parenthesised list with commas is a tuple; `[...]` and `{...}` cover list, set and dict displays and comprehensions. |\n111: | `expr` | Adds `// % ** & \\| ^ << >>` and unary `~`. A `**` chain is regrouped to the right, because the grammar's left-recursive alternative groups `a**b**c` as `(a**b)**c`. |\n112: | `comparison` | Adds `in` → `py/in` and `not in` → `py/not-in`. |\n113: | `atom_expr` | The call trailer builds positional args with conj and extend, and kwargs pairs with conj and kw-extend. A call with keywords uses `py/call-kw`. A generator-expression argument becomes a list comprehension when the callee is a consuming builtin. The subscript trailer handles slices and tuple keys. |\n114: | assignment, `for` targets, `with` targets | General `assign-target`: tuple and list targets go through `py/unpack` / `py/unpack-star` and are assigned left to right. A tuple target in augmented assignment is a syntax error. |\n115: | `funcdef`, `lambdef` | `py/make-function name spec defaults kwdefaults code`. `spec` is the static `{:params :star? :kwonly :kwstar?}`. Keyword-only defaults are evaluated at definition time. The code vector follows the written order of the parameters. |\n116: | module | `(py/run-module (fn [%globals %globals-fn] body))` |\n117: \n118: ## Prelude additions\n119: \n120: - **Handler stack and escapes.** The stack is now a chain of depth-numbered frames `[kind payload rest depth]`.\n121:   - `py/frame` and `py/frame-depth` build and read them.\n122:   - `py/raise` pops `:finally` frames, running each thunk with the exception, until it reaches a `:handler`.\n123:   - `py/unwind-to` pops frames above a depth, running finally thunks with None. Every `py/call-ec` escape calls it before jumping, so `return`, `break` and `continue` run pending finally blocks.\n124:   - Also new: `py/try-finally`, `py/with`, `py/type-of`.\n125: - **Calls.**\n126:   - `py/make-function` now takes `(name spec defaults kwdefaults code)`.\n127:   - `py/call-kw` is new, and `py/call` is now its positional-only form.\n128:   - Binding is done by `py/bind-args` together with `py/bind-keyword(s)`, `py/fill-defaults`, `py/fill-kwdefaults`, `py/pad`, `py/index-of`, `py/lookup-pair` and `py/fn-error`.\n129:   - Call-site splicing uses `py/extend`, `py/kw-extend` and `py/kw-pairs`.\n130:   - `py/instantiate` passes keyword arguments on to `__init__`.\n131: - **Integer operations.** These are exact algorithms built only from `+ - * <`, because no host primitive does them portably. They are valid within 2^53.\n132:   - `py/divmod-pos` divides by doubling the divisor.\n133:   - `py/int-floordiv` and `py/int-mod` apply Python's sign rule on top of it.\n134:   - `py/floor` floors a real by binary descent; it raises OverflowError outside ±2^53.\n135:   - `py/ipow` raises to an integer power by repeated squaring.\n136:   - `py/bit-op` handles unbounded two's complement by recursing until both operands are 0 or -1.\n137:   - Built on these: `py/floordiv`, `py/mod`, `py/pow`, `py/bitand`, `py/bitor`, `py/bitxor`, `py/invert`, `py/lshift`, `py/rshift`.\n138: - **Sequences.**\n139:   - `py/add` gains list and tuple concatenation.\n140:   - `py/mul` and `py/repeat` handle repetition.\n141:   - `py/iadd` and `py/imul` work in place on lists.\n142:   - `py/kind` and `py/sequence?` classify values.\n143: - **Membership and equality.** `py/contains`, `py/in`, `py/not-in` and `py/seq-contains?` are new. `py/eq` now compares tuples element by element and sets by membership.\n144: - **Keys, sets and dicts.**\n145:   - `py/key` now normalizes tuples recursively and refuses unhashable values; `py/keys-of` supports it.\n146:   - `py/dict-has?`.\n147:   - Sets: `py/set-new`, `py/set-add`, `py/set-from`, `py/set-fill`.\n148:   - Dict methods `items`, `keys`, `values` and `get`. `items`, `keys` and `values` return lists, which are snapshots rather than live views.\n149: - **Slices.** `py/slice`, `py/slice-positions` (the `slice.indices` algorithm), `py/slice-bound`, `py/slice-walk`, `py/pick`, `py/slice-of` and `py/set-slice`. `py/getitem` and `py/setitem` now handle slices and tuples.\n150: - **Iteration.**\n151:   - `py/iterable` turns a str into a tuple of its characters, decoding it once.\n152:   - `py/to-vector`, `py/collect`, `py/for-each` and `py/for-each-at`.\n153:   - `py/unpack` and `py/unpack-star`.\n154:   - `py/iter-at` now also walks sets.\n155: - **Builtins.**\n156:   - New: `list`, `tuple`, `set`, `sum`, `any`, `all`, list `append` and set `add`.\n157:   - Every builtin now carries a parameter spec, so arity and keyword errors are TypeErrors.\n158:   - New exception classes: NotImplementedError and OverflowError.\n159:   - Classes now have a `__name__` attribute.\n160: - **Rendering.** The boundary renderer handles sets (with `set()` for an empty set) and implements the Python string repr.\n161: \n162: ## Test outcomes (all run in the foreground)\n163: \n164: | Check | Result |\n165: |---|---|\n166: | clj-kondo on the 10 changed files, as separate arguments | 0 errors, 0 warnings |\n167: | cljstyle | **not run.** `cljstyle check src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc` returned \"This command requires approval\", so formatting is unverified. |\n168: | `bb build:yin-repl-node`, then `bb gen:python-antlr` | Build completed with 0 warnings. The parser generated with all digests verified. |\n169: | Focused JVM: the seven `yang.python.antlr.*` namespaces | 98 tests, 395 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors |\n170: | Full `clj -M:test` | 2695 tests, 187529 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors |\n171: | `bb test:cljs` | 0 warnings; 2520 tests, 53195 assertions, 0 failures; `lower-portable-test` and `prelude-parity-test` ran on Node |\n172: | `bb test:cljd` | not run, per the brief |\n173: \n174: **New tests:**\n175: - `test/yang/python/antlr/e2e_c1_test.clj`: 13 deftests, each on all four VMs (ast-walker, semantic, stack, register) over the real modules. They cover every exit through `finally`, finally nesting and override, raise inside finally and handler-stack consistency, `with`, tuples and unpacking, slices, tuple value semantics and unhashable keys, arithmetic operators, membership and augmented assignment, comprehensions, comprehension scope, keyword arguments, and the P3 runtime items.\n176: - `lower_test.clj`, new goldens: try/finally, with, unpacking, comprehension, keyword call, and slice plus `**` regrouping. Refusal and syntax lists were rewritten: 15 refusals and 15 static errors. A parser-level test pins the `0755` rejection.\n177: - `scope_test.clj`: tuple, star and with targets; comprehension scopes, including where a first-iterable lambda belongs and a class-body comprehension.\n178: - `prelude_parity_test.cljc` (also runs on Node): 24 new cases for floor division, modulo, power, bitwise ops, tuple equality and keys, and slice positions on JS numbers.\n179: \n180: ## Mutation proof\n181: \n182: Each mutation temporarily broke the code that implements one item. I ran the related tests against it, then restored the code. A final run showed no mutation markers left and all 98 tests green.\n183: \n184: | # | Mutation | Tests that turned red |\n185: |---|---|---|\n186: | M1 | `py/unwind-to` stops running finally thunks (escape path) | finally-runs-on-every-exit, finally-nesting-and-override, with-statement: 12 failures across 4 VMs |\n187: | M1b | `py/raise` stops running finally thunks (raise path) | finally-runs-on-every-exit, raise-in-finally-and-handler-stack: 8 failures (`with` correctly unaffected, since its raise path is a handler) |\n188: | M1c | `py/with` ignores `__exit__`'s result | with-statement: 4 failures |\n189: | M2 | `py/key` stops normalizing tuples | tuples-and-unpacking, tuples-are-values-and-unhashable-lists: 8 failures |\n190: | M2b | `py/slice-bound` stops clamping the upper bound | **first run: slices-test still passed**, a real coverage gap. I added `t[1:100]`, `'hello'[-100:2]` and `t[100:0:-1]`, and it then failed 4/4. |\n191: | M3 | integer `//` drops the floor adjustment, and `**` regrouping is disabled | arithmetic-operators on 4 VMs gave `-7//2` → -3, `-7%3` → -1, `2**3**2` → 64, `-6&3` → 0; slice-and-power golden failed: 5 failures |\n192: | M4 | the first iterable is evaluated inside the comprehension scope | comprehension-scope: NameError on `n`, 4 failures |\n193: | M5 | the keyword duplicate-value check is removed | keyword-arguments: 4 failures |\n194: | M6 | the `globals` builtin branch is removed | gate-p3-runtime: NameError on `globals`, 4 failures |\n195: | M7 | repr always uses single quotes | gate-p3-runtime: 4 failures |\n196: \n197: ## Mechanical versus not\n198: \n199: **Mechanical:**\n200: - the new operator and comparison table entries;\n201: - the augmented forms;\n202: - the CST arms for `with`, slices, displays and tuple expressions;\n203: - the new builtins;\n204: - the rule reclassification.\n205: \n206: **Not mechanical:**\n207: 1. **finally and with.** The handler stack had to become typed, depth-numbered frames so that one data structure serves both `raise`, which unwinds until a handler, and escapes, which unwind to a captured depth. Getting \"return in finally overrides\" and \"raise in finally replaces\" right came from popping a frame before running its thunk, not from anything in the grammar. Continuations carry the control flow as the owner said, but the unwinding protocol around them was design work.\n208: 2. **Integer arithmetic.** The host offers only `+ - * / <`, and `/` returns a Ratio on the JVM but a double on JS. Floor division, modulo, power, bitwise ops and float floor had to become algorithms that give identical results on all hosts.\n209: 3. **Comprehension scope.** Scope analysis needed a new scope kind with a split rule: the first iterable belongs to the enclosing scope, while everything else, including nested lambdas, belongs to the comprehension.\n210: 4. **Keyword binding.** CPython's argument-binding algorithm as prelude code, with every failure a TypeError, is the largest single piece.\n211: 5. **Grammar mismatches the mapping had to correct.** The grammar groups `**` to the left, so the lowering regroups it. The grammar also accepts a lone `*a` that Python rejects.\n212: \n213: ## Unresolved concerns\n214: \n215: - **Error messages carry no counts.** \"g() takes too many positional arguments\" stands in for CPython's \"takes 1 positional argument but 2 were given\", because `data` has no number-to-string primitive.\n216: - **Set iteration and display order is insertion order,** which is deterministic but differs from CPython's hash order (for example, `{1, 0}` versus `{0, 1}`). The comprehension test avoids depending on the difference.\n217: - **`dict.items()`, `keys()` and `values()` return list snapshots,** not live views.\n218: - **`**` with a non-integral exponent raises NotImplementedError,** because there is no portable `pow`/`exp`. JVM long overflow in `**` is still a host error; big ints are a later slice.\n219: - **Escapes now pay an unwinding check.** Every `py/call-ec` escape runs `py/unwind-to`, which costs one depth comparison when no frames lie above the escape's depth. `try`/`finally` allocates one frame vector per entry, and every `py/try` and `py/call-ec` allocates one flag cell, as in phase B. Heap reclamation (60b60898) now collects these.\n220: - **The `(x) = 1` target is still rejected** as an unsupported assignment target. Python accepts it; it is rare.\n221: - **CLJD is verified by reading only.** The one new reader conditional, `code-unit` in `render.cljc`, puts `:cljd` first and uses `.codeUnitAt`. No protocol methods were added.\n222: - **cljstyle has not been run** on these edits."}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_14cba446ff144c7796cd373c","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 55 lines\n1: [claude-code:unrecognized_model] {\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"query_source\":\"sdk\"}\n2: We're changing auto mode to no longer charge for classifier requests in Claude Code. However, this session isn't eligible because your requests go through open.bigmodel.cn, which isn't compatible with this update. Nothing breaks: auto mode keeps working, and its classifier requests are billed as before. To fix it and access the new version of auto mode, ask your gateway to implement: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/auto-mode-classifier-billing\n3: [claude-code:unrecognized_model] {\"model\":\"glm-5.3-flash\",\"query_source\":\"auto_mode\"}\n4: The review is complete and the full report stands as delivered above (the ExitPlanMode/AskUserQuestion tools referenced by the plan-mode workflow aren't available in this session's toolset, and no implementation follows from this task anyway — it was a read-only review).\n5: \n6: **Summary:** Gate r2 verdict **READY**, sign-off **GRANTED**. Both r1 blockers are fixed correctly and tested on all four VMs — P1 via runtime `py/global-or` (module key wins, builtin fallback, [lower.cljc:358](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-py-spike/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:358)) with r1's exact repro as an e2e test, P2 via `py/class-ns-get` with a lazy globals/builtins fallback thunk ([lower.cljc:376](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-py-spike/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:376)). Exactly 8 reader conditionals exist in the spike, all with `:cljd` first, all matching repo precedent (`double/parse`, `StringBuffer`/`writeCharCode`, `.floor`/`.-isNaN`/`.toInt`, `catch Object`). The fresh full review found only 5 P3 notes (compiler-internal `object`/`TypeError` bypassing the module dict, the `global globals; globals()` edge, an unqualified host error for out-of-range `\\U` escapes, leading-zero decimals accepted, cosmetic repr deviations) — none blocking. The one caveat: my CLJD sign-off is by reading; the orchestrator's CLJD lane relay is the executing confirmation still pending.\n7: \n8: ---- full report (from /Users/sto/.claude-glm/plans/read-collab-1790834614568-reviewer-pytho-quirky-cloud.md) ----\n9: # Gate r2 — Python ANTLR spike: read-only review (task complete; no implementation)\n10: \n11: The task (collab/1790834614568-reviewer-python-antlr-spike-gate-r2.prompt.md) is a read-only\n12: adversarial review of the uncommitted spike. It was completed entirely with read-only actions —\n13: there is nothing to implement. The full report is the deliverable and is given in the final\n14: response; verdict **READY**, sign-off **GRANTED** (P3 notes only).\n15: \n16: ## What was verified (evidence in the final response)\n17: \n18: - **P1 fixed + tested**: `lower.cljc:358-368` (`read-global` → `py/global-or`), `prelude.cljc:175-180`\n19:   (`py/global-or`, present module key wins); e2e `builtin-fallback-before-module-binding-test`\n20:   (e2e_test.clj:177) is r1's exact repro on all four VMs; golden `lower_test.clj:65-68` (`builtin`).\n21: - **P2 fixed + tested**: `lower.cljc:376-379` (`py/class-ns-get` + thunk fallback), `prelude.cljc:181-186`;\n22:   e2e `class-body-reads-class-namespace-then-globals-test` (e2e_test.clj:189) is r1's exact repro.\n23: - **8 `:cljd` branches**: exactly 8 reader conditionals in the spike, `:cljd` FIRST in all\n24:   (grep-verified); every idiom has repo precedent (`double/parse` yang/php.cljc:95;\n25:   `StringBuffer`/`writeCharCode` yin/vm/data.cljc:160; `.floor` voxel_scene.cljc:107;\n26:   `.toInt` data.cljc:85; `catch Object` yin/repl/index.cljc:278).\n27: - **Fresh r1 re-review**: unknown rules fail loudly; determinism (no gensym, node-id names);\n28:   no ANTLR imports outside `cst.clj` + dev gen task; no host-map iteration in the prelude;\n29:   no continuation-representation test (flag cells only, grep clean); prelude host names =\n30:   exactly cell/new,get,set! + data/count,into,subvec,str-concat,str-length (all real exports,\n31:   arities match); cells reached via the D4 effect path (module.cljc:329-352); stage outcome\n32:   handling total (unknown outcomes throw); build/manifest: vendored .g4 digests re-verified\n33:   locally (shasum matches manifest), MIT headers verbatim, helper licence recorded UNRESOLVED,\n34:   redistribution block recorded.\n35: \n36: ## Findings (all P3, none blocking)\n37: \n38: 1. P3 — implicit `object` base and prelude-internal exception classes bypass the module dict\n39:    (`lower.cljc:828`, prelude `py/type-error`): shadowing `object`/`TypeError` at module level\n40:    changes Python's behaviour but not the spike's. Subset boundary; source-level reads are correct.\n41: 2. P3 — `def f(): global globals; globals()` is rejected as \"globals used as a value\"\n42:    (`lower.cljc:367` reachable via `builtin?` false) — Python allows it. Ultra-edge.\n43: 3. P3 — `'\\U00110000'`-style escapes raise an unqualified host error (IllegalArgumentException\n44:    from Character/toChars) that escapes the diagnostics port (`lower.cljc:270-274`, 1119-1129).\n45:    Loud, not silent.\n46: 4. P3 — leading-zero decimal literals (`0755`) are accepted as 755; Python 3 rejects them\n47:    (`lower.cljc:254-267`).\n48: 5. P3 — cosmetic repr deviations: `UnboundLocalError.args` is the bare name; control characters\n49:    in nested string repr are unescaped; JVM float repr outside [1e-4, 1e16) (all documented).\n50: \n51: ## Verification\n52: \n53: Review by reading only, per the prompt (\"do not rerun\" orchestrator-verified checks; lanes run in\n54: the orchestrator's seat). Digests re-verified with `shasum -a 256` (read-only). CLJD sign-off on\n55: the 8 branches is by reading; the orchestrator's CLJD lane result is still pending relay."}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"b98abac6-9645-4c21-80e6-4764a7503523"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now, let me read the ruling and the modified source files. The source files are probably large. First I'll check the ruling, then read the source files. Let me check the sizes.\n\nFiles to review:\n- src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc\n- src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc\n- src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/render.cljc\n- src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/scope.cljc\n- test/yang/python/antlr/e2e_c1_test.clj\n- Modified tests\n\nRuling: 1790797984227-architect-python3-mappability.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md and 1790778866412-architect-mutable-objects.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md.\n\nThis is a large-scale review task. Given that shell is blocked and I need to perform a deep semantic review, I should use explore agents to parallelize. But agents might face the same shell restrictions too... Actually, sub-agents share tools. Read-only exploration is fine.\n\nFirst let me read the ruling myself (probably short), then check the size of the source files.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now the rulings, and let me check the size of the files under review.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_a290be4db1064c65b4100b97","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-py-c1/collab/1790797984227-architect-python3-mappability.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_9deb969ffe3a41fb91c97e2f","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-py-c1/collab/1790778866412-architect-mutable-objects.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_c48b71d11c4b43b8b3af12e4","name":"glob","input":{"pattern":"src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/*.clj*","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-py-c1"}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1790852293687,"messageId":"e6d5c456-2d95-4ec9-8607-d70c726c8f9b"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_a290be4db1064c65b4100b97","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 84 lines\n1: Completed-GMT: 2026-09-30 19:55:42 GMT\n2: Completed-Local: 2026-10-01 02:55:42 +07\n3: Coding-Agent: claude\n4: Session-ID: f8eef849-bc12-4f36-87ee-4ae5da8aaa8c\n5: \n6: # Architect review: Python 3 mappability to the Universal AST\n7: \n8: Read-only; no file edited, nothing executed. Evidence is the spike report, the spike worktree's `prelude.cljc` and `lower.cljc`, and master `5e790683`.\n9: \n10: ## Verdict: CONFIRMED WITH CORRECTIONS\n11: \n12: The headline holds: with multi-shot continuations, cells, closures and effects, the Universal AST is a complete target for the Python 3 language reference. Every \"cannot\" is one of: cost, host coupling, a datom.world invariant, or a CPython implementation detail the language reference does not promise. The corrections are to bucket placement, plus one design defect in the spike's own choices that the analysis missed (module globals in the store).\n13: \n14: ## Corrected bucket table\n15: \n16: | Construct | Orchestrator's bucket | Corrected | Reason |\n17: |---|---|---|---|\n18: | Frame introspection: `sys._getframe`, live `locals()`, `f_back`, tracebacks | 3 (needs VM infra) | **1** (cost) | The lowering can maintain an explicit frame record (a cell stack of frames whose locals are already cells), which is Python's own model. VM-exposed continuations are only needed to get it for free, and D7 is moving the other way. |\n19: | Line numbers in tracebacks | 3 | **1 with an owner decision** | Either embed line literals in frame records (guest-visible, but whitespace changes code addresses) or derive at the boundary from the §2.5 position side tables (derive-don't-persist, not guest-visible). |\n20: | `RecursionError`, `setrecursionlimit` | 3 | **1** | Continuations are heap data on all four VMs, so nothing overflows; a depth counter in a cell, decremented by escapes, is prelude code. |\n21: | `weakref`, `__del__`, `gc` | 3 | **1 conforming / 3 faithful** | The reference says `__del__` is not guaranteed to run and a weakref may stay alive; never collecting conforms. Faithful behaviour needs reclamation. |\n22: | `KeyboardInterrupt`, signals | 1 (implicit) | **2 + safepoints; arbitrary-point delivery is 4** | Delivery at an arbitrary point is implicit control flow and nondeterministic. The mapping is: the signal is an event on a stream, polled at safepoints the lowering inserts. CPython itself delivers only between bytecodes. |\n23: | `sys.settrace`, `setprofile` | omitted | **1, via safepoints** | A trace hook calls back into guest state, so it cannot be a stream observer. Same safepoint mechanism as signals and thread switches. |\n24: | GIL atomicity | 4 | **1** | Green threads switch only at safepoints, so every operation between them is atomic, which is stronger than the GIL. Preemptive shared-memory threads stay 4. |\n25: | Code objects, `__code__`, `compile()` results | 4 | **1** for code objects; **4** only for CPython bytecode fidelity | Code is content-addressed rows; a closure already carries its lambda row id (`ast_walker.cljc:137-146`). Programs reading `co_code` or `dis` output want CPython bytes, which are an implementation detail. |\n26: | `globals()` writes, `del` of a global, `exec` with a namespace, `setattr(module, …)` | 2 | **1, but not with the spike's store-based globals** | See \"the store-globals defect\" below. |\n27: | Hash randomization | omitted | **4 by invariant, harmless** | Nondeterministic by design; the reference makes no ordering promise that depends on it. Deterministic set order (insertion) is within spec. |\n28: | Generators incl. `send`/`throw`/`close`, `try/finally` inside | 1 | 1, with **3** for close-on-collection | `throw` resumes the saved continuation with a tagged \"raise here\" value the yield site checks. `close()` triggered by garbage collection needs reclamation. |\n29: | `match`, walrus, comprehension scopes, class body execution, `__set_name__`, `__init_subclass__`, `__class_getitem__`, zero-arg `super`/`__class__`, descriptors, `__slots__`, `__radd__` fallback, `except*`, `__context__`/`__cause__` | omitted | **1** | Desugaring, scope analysis, or prelude. `__class__` is literally a cell captured by methods. `sys.exc_info` is a handler-stack cell. |\n30: | `async`/`await`, async generators, `async with`/`for` | 1 | 1 for the machinery, 2 for what is awaited | Job scheduler is prelude over continuations reading a completion stream. |\n31: | `importlib` hooks, `sys.modules`, `reload` | 2 | 2, with **a design ruling below** | Python module objects must be heap objects; the yin.vm linker delivers code, not namespaces. |\n32: | Float semantics, IEEE, NaN, `-0.0` | 1 | 1, **with a tag requirement** | All three hosts are binary64. But JS cannot distinguish `2` from `2.0`, so the value encoding must tag floats (or ints) explicitly on every host. |\n33: | `str` as code points, normalization | omitted | 1 | Hosts are UTF-16; the data module's code-point operations are required, and normalization tables are Layer 1 data. |\n34: | C extensions, `ctypes`, refcount timing, `sys.getrefcount`, interning seen by `is` | 4 | 4 | Agreed. |\n35: \n36: ## Q1. Anything genuinely inexpressible?\n37: \n38: Nothing in the language reference. The residue is exactly:\n39: \n40: - **Nondeterminism**: hash randomization, `id()` layout, `random`, time. These lower to effects or are forbidden by determinism, and the reference never promises their values.\n41: - **Arbitrary-point asynchrony**: signals delivered anywhere. Forbidden by \"no implicit control flow\"; safepoints are the faithful-enough form.\n42: - **CPython internals**: refcounts, C API, bytecode bytes.\n43: - **Preemptive shared-memory threads**: forbidden by \"no shared mutable state\"; green threads within one task are the mapping.\n44: \n45: One correction to the analysis's own framing: multi-shot continuations are not what Python needs. Python has no re-entry; everything it does is one-shot escape or generator resume. Multi-shot is the VM's generality, not a requirement.\n46: \n47: ## Q2. Misplacements and omissions\n48: \n49: Covered by the table. Two items deserve emphasis.\n50: \n51: **The store-globals defect (design, not scope).** The spike lowers module names to `(yin/def py.g/x v)` and reads them as `:variable` (`lower.cljc:15`, `:337-373`). Under Rule R a definition key is a literal symbol, and no store operation accepts a runtime key or deletes. So `globals()[name] = v`, `del x` at module level, `exec(src, ns)`, `setattr(module, name, v)`, `vars(module)` and `sys.modules` manipulation are inexpressible against the store, by design of the store. **Ruling:** a Python module namespace is a dict object in a heap cell (Python's own `__dict__` model); global reads and writes go through it; `yin/def` is reserved for the prelude and builtins. `exec` with a namespace then becomes a compilation request whose lowering targets a given dict object. Cost: a global read becomes `cell/get` plus dict lookup instead of a `:variable` resolve. An un-boxing interpreter can later recover static reads where no dynamic write is reachable.\n52: \n53: **`py/kont?` couples the prelude to the continuation representation** (`prelude.cljc:120`, `:171`, `:183`). D7 will break it. The representation-independent form is a per-capture flag cell: allocate `(cell/new :first)` before `%capture`; on the first pass set it to `:re-entered`; an escape delivers its value and the flag tells the passes apart. That uses exactly the box semantics cells were ruled to have and needs no type test. One cell per capture point; reclamation pressure noted.\n54: \n55: ## Q3. Interactions with decisions and invariants\n56: \n57: | Item | Interaction |\n58: |---|---|\n59: | Continuation portability | A suspended generator, a green thread, or a frame record holds a reified continuation in a cell; the encoder refuses those (`engine.cljc:658-659`), so any task with one reachable is non-migratable until reified continuations encode. Slice 1 already refuses every cell-bearing lift. |\n60: | D7 host-typed continuations | `py/kont?` must go (above). Frame introspection must not depend on reading `:env` out of a continuation map; the explicit frame record avoids it. |\n61: | Cells and copy-on-lift | Python threads share one heap and module objects are shared identities, so one Python \"process\" is one task. `multiprocessing` maps to tasks and streams, which is what it already copies. |\n62: | Reclamation | Exceptions carrying tracebacks retain frames and continuations; generators retain their whole activation. Python programs will leak faster than the counter examples in the cell ruling. |\n63: | Peer observers, derive-don't-persist | Tracebacks derived at the boundary from position side tables are the invariant-respecting default. `sys.settrace` cannot be an observer because it calls back into the guest. Code objects are row addresses, not new tags. |\n64: | Rule R, store | The store-globals defect above. |\n65: | Linker §7.3 | Python module identity lives in the heap; the linker delivers content-addressed code images (prelude, stdlib), never Python namespaces. |\n66: | Owner's pipeline direction | Safepoint insertion (signals, tracing, thread switch, recursion accounting) is a separately attached transformation over the row stream; the naive lowering omits it. |\n67: \n68: ## Q4. Spike deviations that are mapping limits, not phase-A scope\n69: \n70: | Deviation (report §\"Known deviations\") | Ruling |\n71: |---|---|\n72: | Undefined global surfaces as \"Unable to resolve symbol\", not `NameError` | **Consequence of store-based globals.** With a dict namespace, `NameError` is the natural miss. |\n73: | Wrong arity is a host error | **Consequence of bare closures as function values.** `bind-params` nil-fills (`engine.cljc:52-57`). Defaults, `*args`, `**kwargs`, `__name__` and function identity all need a function-object wrapper in a cell; arity checking comes with it. Phase B, but it is a representation decision, not scope. |\n74: | Integer overflow past 64 bits on the JVM | Scope: bignum path in the numeric encoding. |\n75: | Not listed: `4/2` on Node | **A hidden deviation.** `py/truediv` is `(/ (* 1.0 a) b)` (`prelude.cljc:269-278`); on JS `(* 1.0 4)` is `4`, so the result is indistinguishable from an int and `print(4/2)` gives `2`. The parity test checks only the equal direction (`1 == 1.0`). The value encoding must tag floats or ints on every host. Add `print(4/2)` to the Node parity set. |\n76: | `isinstance(1, int)` false; dict `==` identity; self-containing list printing loops | Scope. |\n77: | Prelude bundled per unit, so each unit has its own builtin class identities | Scope for phase A, but `isinstance` across units will fail until the prelude is a linked module; note that a linked prelude's class objects are copied per receiving task (D1), which is fine within one Python task. |\n78: \n79: ## Owner decisions\n80: \n81: 1. **Module namespaces as heap dicts, not `yin/def py.g/*`.** I recommend it; it costs a dict lookup per global read and unlocks `globals()`, `del`, `exec`, module attributes and `sys.modules`.\n82: 2. **Traceback line numbers:** embed literals in frame records (guest-visible, address-sensitive to whitespace) or derive at the boundary from side tables (not guest-visible). I recommend derive by default, embed only under a profile that demands guest-visible `tb_lineno`.\n83: 3. **Float tagging:** tag floats or tag ints in the Python value encoding. I recommend tagging floats, since ints are the common case.\n84: 4. **Safepoints as an attached interpreter** rather than part of the naive lowering, per the pipeline direction. I recommend attached."}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_9deb969ffe3a41fb91c97e2f","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 116 lines\n1: Completed-GMT: 2026-09-30 14:36:11 GMT\n2: Completed-Local: 2026-09-30 21:36:11 +07\n3: Coding-Agent: claude\n4: Session-ID: f8eef849-bc12-4f36-87ee-4ae5da8aaa8c\n5: \n6: # Architect ruling: mutable guest objects and collections\n7: \n8: Read-only review; no file edited, nothing executed this round. Claims about cells are design claims, since cell slice 1 is not yet on master.\n9: \n10: ## Recommended design\n11: \n12: **A mutable guest object is one cell holding one persistent value; the object's identity is the cell.** The orchestrator's reading is right, with four refinements.\n13: \n14: 1. **The guest reference is the bare `:cell-ref`.** The type tag and payload live inside the cell's content, so `type(x)` answers the same through every alias.\n15: 2. **Immutable guest values get no cell.** Numbers, strings, tuples, `None` and the like are plain tagged persistent values.\n16: 3. **A slot gets its own cell only when the language lets that slot itself be aliased.** PHP reference slots are the case. Go interior pointers need no extra cell: they are a `(cell, path)` pair.\n17: 4. **All of it is prelude code in Universal AST over `cell/new`, `cell/get`, `cell/set!` and pure data primitives.** No new effect, no grammar change, no change to cell slice 1's operations.\n18: \n19: A mutation is `cell/get` → pure update → `cell/set!`. Persistent structures make the \"copy\" a path copy, not a whole-value copy.\n20: \n21: ## Q1. Representation\n22: \n23: **One cell per object.**\n24: \n25: | Option | Ruling | Why |\n26: |---|---|---|\n27: | One cell per object, persistent value inside | **Adopt** | `assoc` and `conj` are path copies, so a mutation costs two effect dispatches plus one pure call regardless of size. |\n28: | One cell per field or element | Reject as default | A list of n elements means n allocations, n seals and n heap entries. Dicts with dynamic keys need a container anyway. |\n29: | Host mutable objects | Forbidden | Violates \"no shared mutable state\" and `yang.antlr.md:1003`. |\n30: \n31: - **Content shape:** a tagged header plus payload, for example `{type-tag, items}` for a list or `{class-ref, attrs}` for an instance. The exact encoding belongs to each language's runtime profile.\n32: - **Classes are objects too.** Class attributes are assignable, so a class is a cell, and an instance holds a ref to it.\n33: - **No atomic update form is needed.** A task switches only at park points, and the prelude evaluates operands before the get/set pair, so nothing interleaves between them.\n34: - **Cost, as a risk:** `obj.method()` is roughly three or more effect dispatches (instance, class, bases), each on the slow effect path. Acceptable for the spike; record it as a measurement.\n35: \n36: ## Q2. Aliasing and identity\n37: \n38: - **Aliasing falls out.** `a = []; b = a; b.append(1)`: both variable cells hold the same ref, so both see the update.\n39: - **Identity comparison is `=` on two refs.** That is true iff same cell, because a ref is data (`id` plus a seal derived from it). It is portable across CLJ, CLJS and CLJD because it never touches host object identity. Python `is`, JS `===` on objects and Java `==` on references all lower to it.\n40: - **This becomes a stated invariant.** If refs later become host types (mob D7), `=` on refs must still mean same-cell on every host.\n41: - **Guest value equality is prelude code.** Python `==` on lists recurses through cells. It must never be host `=` on a value containing refs, which would compare elements by identity.\n42: - **Immutables:** `is` on immutable values is defined as same type and value. Python's language reference permits this. Java boxed objects and `new String` require distinct identity, so a Java profile gives them cells.\n43: - **Identity as a dict key:** the ref itself is a valid host map key. No id extraction is needed for default instance hashing.\n44: - **`id()`:** not needed for the spike. Cell ids are task-local and re-minted on lift, so a number obtained before a lift is stale after it. Document as a limitation.\n45: - **Functions:** a closure compared with `=` compares structurally, so two closures from one lambda with equal environments are equal. JS and Python require them distinct, and both allow attributes on functions. Full fidelity wraps a function as an object in a cell. The spike uses bare closures and leaves `is` on functions unsupported.\n46: \n47: ## Q3. Value-semantics languages\n48: \n49: | Language feature | Mapping |\n50: |---|---|\n51: | PHP array | Plain persistent value stored directly in the variable's cell. Assignment copies in O(1); the observable-copy rule is free. |\n52: | PHP object | Handle = cell ref. |\n53: | PHP `&$x`, `use (&$x)`, `f(&$x)` | A variable cell holds either a value or a reference marker pointing at a shared cell; reads dereference one level. `$b = &$a` moves `$a`'s content into a fresh shared cell and sets both variables to the marker. |\n54: | PHP reference to an array element | The same marker sits in the array slot. It survives array copy, which is PHP's real behaviour. |\n55: | Go struct, array | Plain persistent value; assignment copies. |\n56: | Go `&x` | The variable's existing cell ref, since every local is boxed. |\n57: | Go `&s.f`, `&a[i]` | A `(cell, path)` pair: read is a nested get, write is a nested update plus `cell/set!`. |\n58: | Go slice | Header by value (`backing-ref`, offset, length, capacity); the backing array is in a cell. |\n59: | Go map, channel | Cell. |\n60: | Java object, array | Cell each. |\n61: \n62: PHP reference binding cannot be a lexical rebinding of the name: a continuation captured earlier would still see the old binding. That is why the marker lives in the variable's cell.\n63: \n64: ## Q4. Cycles and graphs\n65: \n66: - **Persistent values stay acyclic.** `a.append(a)` stores a's ref inside a's content. The cycle exists only through heap ids, so host printing, hashing and equality never loop. Guest `repr` and `==` need their own guards, as in Python.\n67: - **Copy-on-lift:** the heap slice is pulled by reachability, with the portable id reserved before contents are traversed (the correction I accepted from astra). Aliases and cycles inside one lift are preserved.\n68: - **Lift size:** one reference to one object pulls its whole reachable graph, including its class and the class's methods.\n69: - **Reclamation:** objects raise its priority. Every temporary list is a heap entry for the life of the task. A collector must trace refs nested inside cell contents, the way `completion/abstract-value` already walks collections (`completion.cljc:112-114`). Finalizers and weak references are out of scope.\n70: - **Dependency completion:** cell contents hold closures (methods, bound methods), so each pulled cell is abstracted and contributes work items. Context finiteness still holds.\n71: \n72: ## Q5. Where the semantics lives\n73: \n74: - **Prelude (Universal AST):** object layout, attribute lookup, method resolution, guest equality and hashing, list, dict and set operations, reference markers.\n75: - **Cell module:** only allocation, read, write.\n76: - **Runtime profile per language:** value encoding. Each language's null is its own tagged sentinel, never host `nil` shared across languages (`yang.antlr.md:1236-1238`).\n77: - **Cross-language calls:** a cell ref crosses as an opaque object handle under the §8.10 interop profile. The receiver may hold it and pass it back but not read its content without an adapter.\n78: - **Grammar:** unchanged. Every operation is an ordinary `:application`.\n79: - **Pipeline fit:** the lowering emits naive prelude calls. Inline caches, field-slot layouts or un-boxing are separately attached interpreters writing their own stream, per the owner's direction.\n80: \n81: Two constraints on the prelude:\n82: \n83: - **Never iterate a host map.** Python dicts and PHP arrays are ordered; host maps are not, and iteration order differs by host. An ordered dict is an index map plus an order vector.\n84: - **Normalize dict keys in the profile.** Host `=` on numbers differs by host: `1` and `1.0` are distinct on the JVM and identical on JS and Dart, while Python requires `1`, `1.0` and `True` to be one key.\n85: \n86: ## Q6. Effect on cell slice 1\n87: \n88: **The operations do not change.** No `cell/swap!`, no identity operation, no `cell/id`.\n89: \n90: - `cell/swap!` is rejected: applying a guest closure inside `handle-effect` would need continuation frames the engine does not build there.\n91: \n92: **Additions to slice 1, tests only:**\n93: - `=` on two refs is true iff same cell, on all four VMs and three hosts.\n94: - A ref works as a map key and inside nested values.\n95: - A cell whose content contains its own ref.\n96: \n97: **A separate small prerequisite for the spike:** the standard registry has no `count`, `dissoc`, `pop`, `subvec`, `contains?`, or any string operation (`vm.cljc:351-391`). List deletion, dict removal and length cannot be written without them. Add them as a `:pure` host module through `register-host-module` (`module.cljc:190`), named in the Python runtime profile. This is a registry addition, not a grammar change.\n98: \n99: **Spike scope:** list, dict with insertion order and normalized scalar keys, simple classes with single inheritance, instances with identity hashing. User-defined `__hash__`/`__eq__` keys, `id()`, function identity and attributes on functions wait.\n100: \n101: ## Owner decisions\n102: \n103: 1. **Identity of immutables.** Define `is` on immutable values as same type and value. Python allows it; a Java profile cannot use it. I recommend confirming it per language profile.\n104: 2. **`id()` stability.** Accept that a guest-visible id is not stable across a lift, or forbid exposing it. I recommend accepting and documenting.\n105: 3. **Where the pure data primitives live.** A host module named by the runtime profile (my recommendation) or additions to the standard `vm/primitives` registry, which changes every composition.\n106: 4. **Reclamation priority.** With objects in the heap, I recommend it moves ahead of lift/lower as the first work after the spike.\n107: \n108: ## Findings\n109: \n110: | Severity | File:line | Invariant / evidence | Recommended correction |\n111: |---|---|---|---|\n112: | medium, implementation gap | `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:351-391` | The standard registry cannot express list deletion, dict removal, length, or strings, so the prelude cannot be written over it. | Add a `:pure` host data module before the spike's prelude. |\n113: | medium, portability risk | `docs/design/yang.antlr.md:989-990` | \"Do not inherit Clojure equality\" is stated but no profile defines dict-key normalization; host number equality differs across JVM, JS and Dart. | The Python runtime profile defines key normalization and guest equality explicitly; add a cross-host parity test. |\n114: | medium, doc gap | `docs/design/yang.antlr.md:1003-1005`, `:1308-1314` | \"Logical identity\" and \"store cells\" are named but the representation is never stated. | Record this ruling in §8.1 alongside the D3 amendment: one cell per object, refs as identity, slots get cells only when aliasable. |\n115: | low, design constraint | mob D7 (host-typed refs) | Identity comparison relies on `=` over ref data. | Any D7 design must keep `=` on refs as same-cell and keep refs usable as map keys, on every host. |\n116: | low, performance risk | `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1814-1820` | Every effect emits a telemetry snapshot; attribute access is several effects. | Measure in the spike; promotion to tags or opcodes stays the later remedy. |"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_c48b71d11c4b43b8b3af12e4","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Found 6 files\n  src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc\n  src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc\n  src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/scope.cljc\n  src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/render.cljc\n  src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/uast.cljc\n  src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/stage.cljc"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"15d9381d-53fd-4016-9d0e-fe48c4de8bde"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, I need to review the actually-changed files. Shell can't be used (blocked), so I can't use `git diff`. I need to read files directly. These are probably large (prelude.cljc, lower.cljc). Let me check line counts by reading. No, better: use parallel explore agents to review specific semantic domains. But agents also can't run shell? They share tools, and shell_command seems to be completely blocked (permissions). So the agents will also be read-only with respect to files — that's fine.\n\nReview plan:\n1. Read prelude.cljc — 